WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.801 --> 00:00:07.917
- We have a number of seats
over on this side over here.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.520
There's a row of about five
or six seats on this end.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.720
There are some seats
down here in the front.

00:00:27.357 --> 00:00:30.413
And I see some seats scattered
through here as well.

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:39.930
Why don't you have a seat to,

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.250
have a seat right here.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.340
I'll give you a bit of an introduction

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:44.590
and we'll be ready to go.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:55.553
That's good.

00:01:08.583 --> 00:01:11.160
Good afternoon, ladies and gentlemen.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:12.740
My name is Jeff Brenzel.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.410
I'm the dean of undergraduate
admissions here at Yale,

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:17.980
and also the master of
Timothy Dwight College,

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:20.640
and a lecturer in the
philosophy department.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.640
And I'm the steward of the
Chubb Fellowship at Yale,

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:29.430
and I wanna tell you just a
brief word about the fellowship.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:34.430
In 1949, a gift from
Yale alumnus Hendon Chubb

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.490
launched a speaking fellowship at Yale

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:40.960
to promote the notion that
service to the public good

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.173
is the highest calling to
which any citizen can aspire.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.620
to be named a Chubb Fellow
is Yale's most notable honor

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:51.500
accorded to a visiting speaker.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.130
Chubb fellows have included
many heads of state

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:58.410
and national leaders, Nobel
and Pulitzer Prize winners,

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.490
and a wide range of other
highly accomplished individuals

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:05.400
in political affairs, nonprofit
and for-profit governance,

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.580
the literary and fine arts,

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.430
whose experiences and
leadership have helped define

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:12.940
the challenges of our times,

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.390
and the ways that we should
seek to address them.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:18.310
Before I introduce this spring's

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:22.750
Chubb Fellowship recipient,
President Leonel Fernandez,

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:24.700
I want to take a moment to recognize

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.540
another prominent citizen
of the Dominican Republic

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.400
who has traveled from
Washington to be with us,

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.890
our special guest, the current ambassador

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:35.270
of the Dominican Republic
to the United States

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:36.875
Anibal de Cristo.

00:02:36.875 --> 00:02:37.708
(audience applauding)

00:02:37.708 --> 00:02:39.723
De Castro, Anibal de Castro.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:45.650
Before his government
service, Ambassador de Castro

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:48.060
was a noted Dominican journalist,

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:51.800
ultimately serving as
editor-in-chief of Ultima Hora,

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:54.440
then as director of the newspaper.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.750
He also co-founded Editorial AA,

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.300
publisher of the groundbreaking

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:01.530
socio-political magazine Rumbo.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.390
From 2000 until his appointment in 2004

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:06.470
as ambassador of the Dominican Republic

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:08.180
to the United Kingdom,

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:11.950
he directed the newspaper Diario Libre.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:14.220
After serving for six years as ambassador

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.600
from the Dominican Republic
to the United Kingdom,

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:19.480
he presented his credentials
to President Barack Obama

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.150
as the current Dominican ambassador

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:24.860
to the United States in July of 2011.

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:26.493
Welcome to Yale, Mr. Ambassador.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.530
I also wanted to welcome
the several members

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:35.520
of the president's staff
who are here with us

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.500
for his Chubb Fellowship visit.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:42.500
including especially Yamile Eusebio,

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:46.300
director of the president's
Global Foundation

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.890
for Democracy and Development in New York.

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:50.833
And there's Yamile here, there we go.

00:03:50.833 --> 00:03:54.083
(audience applauding)

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:58.860
Finally, I want to
express the special thanks

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.810
of both Yale and the
Chubb Fellowship Committee

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:05.450
to the Dominican Students
Association of Yale,

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:07.700
and in particular Patricio Brito,

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:10.010
who has worked for over
two years to find a way

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:13.090
to bring President
Fernandez to our campus.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:15.690
I might say that not
only did Patricio's work

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.030
result in a Chubb Fellowship recipient

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:21.730
who is bringing us a remarkable
experience in public life,

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:23.620
and an important message for our time

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:25.540
regarding Latin America,

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:26.970
but this afternoon in a meeting

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:29.020
with the Dominican Students Association,

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:31.732
and also the Latino student organization

00:04:31.732 --> 00:04:33.190
(speaks foreign language)

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:35.690
President Fernandez
and officers and alumni

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:38.340
of the association committed
to an important series

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:42.220
of joint exchanges among
undergraduate and graduate students

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:44.970
of Dominican heritage
in the United States,

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:47.040
and the work especially in education

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.690
to which the president's
foundation is now committed.

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:52.360
So now let me introduce briefly

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:55.780
our distinguished Chubb
Fellowship visiting speaker

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:58.610
President Leonel Fernandez,

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:00.190
who this past summer stepped down

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:01.870
from his third term of office

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:04.863
as the elected president
of the Dominican Republic.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:08.220
He was born there in 1953,

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.750
and came to New York City at the age of 8,

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:14.890
attending school in this country from 1962

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:18.743
until his return to the
Dominican Republic in 1971.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:23.110
In 1978 he graduated from
the Autonomous University

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:26.190
of Santo Domingo with honors in law.

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:28.390
He worked as a teacher and a journalist

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:31.720
and also practiced law
before entering politics

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:34.740
as a follower and protege
of the noted Dominican

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:38.920
thinker writer and
political leader Juan Bosch,

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:41.513
founder of the Dominican Liberation Party.

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:45.650
In 1996. as the presidential
candidate for the party,

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:48.130
Mr. Fernandez survived the first round

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:50.370
of a heavily contested election,

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:53.860
and won the second round
by a narrow margin.

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:57.060
Elected at age 42, he
is the youngest person

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:59.173
ever to have served in the office.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:03.670
Mr. Fernandez vowed to
end political corruption,

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:05.380
implemented closer oversight

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:08.050
of the judiciary police and military,

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:10.870
and promised greater scrutiny
of state-owned firms,

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:15.100
and reforms to strengthen
manufacturing and agriculture.

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:17.280
In 1999, he announced an initiative

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:19.930
to broaden the country's
economic base by attracting

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:23.260
high technology firms to
the Dominican Republic.

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:27.053
In 1998, he restored
diplomatic relations with Cuba.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:30.710
Constitutionally barred from
running for re-election,

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:34.010
Mr. Fernandez left office in 2000.

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:39.010
In 2004 however, he was returned
to office by a wide margin.

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:42.323
and he was re-elected
to a third term in 2008.

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:46.770
In 2011, Mr. Fernandez was pressured

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:49.630
by some of his partisans to pursue

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:52.975
the further amendment of
the country's constitution

00:06:52.975 --> 00:06:56.550
to allow him to serve yet
another consecutive term.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:58.700
He opposed the change to the constitution,

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:01.063
and stepped down from
office this past summer.

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:06.410
Both in office and out,
president Fernandez has sustained

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:09.650
a focus on economic growth
and financial stability

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:12.320
as the keys to both political success

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:13.713
and success of the nation.

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:17.740
It is a fact that growth rates
in the Dominican Republic

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:19.450
during his terms of office

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:22.023
have been among the very
highest in Latin America.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:26.380
He has in addition paid
sustained attention to education,

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:29.180
to food security, to
agricultural development,

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:30.973
and to regional cooperation.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:34.870
As I have spent time with
him over the last two days,

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:37.093
I have noticed something in particular,

00:07:38.433 --> 00:07:40.620
and in particular in his
speaking to students.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:42.970
Both last night at the
Chubb Fellowship dinner

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:44.810
in Timothy Dwight College,

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:46.370
and the students this afternoon

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:48.523
from the latino organizations at Yale.

00:07:50.510 --> 00:07:53.710
One thing I've noted is he's
extraordinarily generous

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:57.023
with his time, and access to his person.

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:00.890
I don't know how many students
he greeted last night,

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:03.190
but it felt like it was
virtually all of them.

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:08.630
Beyond that, he is what I
speaking as a philosopher

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:11.713
would call a pragmatist philosopher.

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:16.030
And we think of pragmatism
generally speaking

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:19.070
in the kind of common everyday
sense as getting things done.

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:20.130
How do you get things done?

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:22.210
You're a pragmatist,
you're not an ideologue.

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:26.243
You work to to make the trains run.

00:08:26.243 --> 00:08:28.293
You get things done.

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:32.710
However, that's not what pragmatism is

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:36.290
as a philosophical movement.

00:08:36.290 --> 00:08:40.036
Philosophically speaking,
pragmatism means that

00:08:40.036 --> 00:08:43.310
the doing is the learning.

00:08:43.310 --> 00:08:45.640
And I have heard him
say this several times

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:48.000
over the past two days to students.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:50.550
That you must get beyond your classrooms,

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:53.730
and you must actually be in contact

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:56.030
with the work that you hope to engage

00:08:56.030 --> 00:09:00.140
in order to learn what it is
that you are going to be doing.

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:04.040
A deeply pragmatic
perspective on education.

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:08.290
However, I will say that
at the very same time,

00:09:08.290 --> 00:09:12.650
he has shown an extraordinary
intellectual curiosity,

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:14.760
both in his description of how

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:18.870
he got interested in
politics and growing up,

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:21.820
the self-taught intellectual culture

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:24.520
that he inhabited both in in New York

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:26.350
and in the Dominican Republic,

00:09:26.350 --> 00:09:29.990
and evidenced in even such things as

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:31.870
when he arrived in town yesterday

00:09:31.870 --> 00:09:33.790
and I thought we'd all go out to lunch,

00:09:33.790 --> 00:09:37.050
he said, "no, I'd actually
like to go to the bookstore."

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:38.480
And all of his staff know this,

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:40.090
we talked about with
the students last night,

00:09:40.090 --> 00:09:41.490
we spent the next hour and a half,

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:43.310
his first hour and a half in New Haven

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:44.730
in the Yale bookstore.

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:46.820
And I think that the take at the end

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:51.610
was about 60 to 80 books
that he was taking back home

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:54.120
to add to the 400,000 books

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:57.573
that he has collected for
his Foundation Institute.

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:02.300
And to and to accompany this
afternoon's conversations

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:05.300
with the students about
intellectual exchanges,

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:08.890
where he shows the other side
of pragmatist philosophy,

00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:13.670
which is that the exchange,
the very exchange of ideas

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:15.420
brings people together.

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:18.820
Gives them a common experience
and a common vocabulary,

00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:22.910
and allows them to transmit
practices and ideas

00:10:22.910 --> 00:10:25.820
and relationships from
one context to the other.

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:30.100
And it was in that
modality that he was hoping

00:10:30.100 --> 00:10:35.100
that this visit to Yale would
initiate exchanges between

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:37.960
the Dominican and other
Latino students here

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:41.140
and what's happening in
the Dominican Republic.

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:43.440
So I would like you to welcome

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:46.460
this deeply and
philosophically pragmatic man

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:48.368
President Leonel Fernandez.

00:10:48.368 --> 00:10:51.618
(audience applauding)

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:02.720
- Thank you, Master Brenzel

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:05.380
for your very kind and warm introduction,

00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:07.420
and thank you for all the generosity

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:09.580
that you've shown in the last two days.

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:11.490
He even took me to a Cuban restaurant

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:12.900
the first day I was here.

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:13.733
Thank you a lot.

00:11:13.733 --> 00:11:14.566
- [Jeff] It was good too.

00:11:14.566 --> 00:11:16.160
- It was very good.

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:18.160
Thank you also for your presence,

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:20.019
Ambassador Anibal de Castro,

00:11:20.019 --> 00:11:21.969
and to all of you for being here today.

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:24.380
Over fifty years ago,

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:27.120
in his commencement speech here at Yale,

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:29.480
President John F. Kennedy began by stating

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:31.830
that he had the best of two world's.

00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:33.963
A Harvard education and a Yale degree.

00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:37.380
Well right from the beginning,

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:40.220
I have to admit openly and honestly

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:42.580
that I have not been so fortunate

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:44.480
in experiencing the wonders of neither

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:46.193
of those two academic worlds.

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:52.100
Nonetheless, it is an honor and
a privilege to be here today

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:55.950
at this prestigious
institution of higher learning,

00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:59.340
from where two prominent
Latinos were educated,

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:03.420
becoming for many a source of inspiration.

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:06.860
Supreme Court Justice Sonia Sotomayor

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:09.870
and former Mexican
President Ernesto Zedillo.

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:12.430
I am also very pleased to know

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:14.500
there is a group of young and talented

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:16.900
Dominican students here at Yale

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:20.720
who have organized themselves since 2005

00:12:21.580 --> 00:12:23.660
in a very creative manner

00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:26.210
within the Dominican Student Association

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:27.263
called QuisqueYalies.

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:33.180
We have Quisqueya, it is the
original name of our country.

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:35.100
So they have mixed Quisqueya with Yale,

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:37.493
calling themselves QuisqueYalies.

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:41.330
Personally, I'm not only delighted

00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:44.100
to find a Dominican and Latino presence

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:47.183
in this very vibrant
intellectual environment,

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:50.440
but also thrilled to share
with you the news that

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:52.770
even though a small nation,

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:55.460
the Dominican Republic, my country,

00:12:55.460 --> 00:13:00.460
very recently gained a respected
status as a world power

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:03.480
in a particular field of interest

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:06.100
and great following here in the US.

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:07.240
Can you get what?

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:08.557
Baseball.

00:13:08.557 --> 00:13:10.650
(audience laughing)

00:13:10.650 --> 00:13:14.133
Just a couple of weeks ago,
the Dominican Republic,

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:16.320
thank you!

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:17.617
(audience laughing)

00:13:17.617 --> 00:13:20.784
(audience applauding)

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:26.293
Just a couple of weeks ago,
the Dominican Republic,

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:29.293
which can also be called baseball land,

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:32.720
became the undefeated champion

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:34.543
of the World Baseball Classic.

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:39.320
And it is with that distinction
and sense of national pride

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:42.160
that I express my gratitude
to Yale's authorities,

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:44.200
faculty members, and students

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:45.570
for inviting me to address you

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:47.107
on the relevance of Latin America

00:13:47.107 --> 00:13:49.363
and the Caribbean in the 21st century.

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:53.510
As you all know, Latin
America and the Caribbean

00:13:53.510 --> 00:13:58.510
represent a region of 33
independent and sovereign states

00:13:58.810 --> 00:14:03.620
extending from the Rio Grande,
this is Texas and New Mexico,

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:05.890
to the southern tip of South America,

00:14:05.890 --> 00:14:09.533
covering an area 2 1/2
times the size of the US.

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:14.020
It accounts for nearly four%
of the Earth's surface,

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:17.020
and can be divided into
several sub regions,

00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:19.090
such as Mesoamerica,

00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:22.290
which includes Mexico and Central America,

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:25.030
the Andeans and Southern Cone countries

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:27.533
in South America and
the Caribbean islands.

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:32.960
At this moment, nearly 600
million people live in the area.

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:37.960
In 1900, the population was
made up of 60 million people.

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:43.010
50 years later in 1950, it
came up to 150 million people,

00:14:43.590 --> 00:14:45.000
which at that time equals

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:47.610
the population of the United States.

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:52.610
Since then, up till now 60
years later, it has quadrupled,

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:56.890
and is almost a double
of current US population.

00:14:56.890 --> 00:15:00.660
About 1/3 or 30% of Latin
America's population

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:02.910
is under 15 years of age,

00:15:02.910 --> 00:15:05.053
so it is a very young population.

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:08.720
Great ethnic and demographic diversity

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:10.760
characterizes the region.

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:12.430
The people of Latin American represent

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:15.160
a mixture of various racial groups

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:18.270
comprised of indigenous
people, white Europeans,

00:15:18.270 --> 00:15:20.463
black Africans, Asians, and Arabs.

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:24.540
Mexico, Central America,
and the Andean regions

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:28.320
in South America have the
largest indigenous population,

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:30.120
which makes up a larger part of the people

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:33.893
living in Bolivia, Ecuador,
Peru, and Guatemala.

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:36.510
Various languages are spoken.

00:15:36.510 --> 00:15:39.680
Spanish, Portuguese,
French, English, Dutch,

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:42.000
and there were 30
different indigenous ones

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:44.133
including Quechua and Aymara.

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:47.200
Latin America has the largest reserves

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:49.390
of arable land in the world.

00:15:49.390 --> 00:15:52.380
It produces oil, metal, foodstuff.

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:54.780
It has an impressive rich biodiversity,

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:57.610
and astonishing natural environments.

00:15:57.610 --> 00:16:00.570
The region is home to
the Amazon rainforest,

00:16:00.570 --> 00:16:04.670
the Galapagos Islands in
Ecuador, and the Andean glaciers.

00:16:04.670 --> 00:16:07.680
It accounts for 23% of
the world's forests,

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:10.513
and 31% of its freshwater resources.

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:15.900
It also houses approximately
70% of the world's species,

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:18.363
and almost 20% of its ecoregions.

00:16:19.330 --> 00:16:21.920
Brazil, the world's fifth largest country

00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:25.390
in area and population, has
more environmental capital

00:16:25.390 --> 00:16:29.083
and more fresh water than
any country in the world.

00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:31.790
The region has a combined GDP

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:36.790
of six 6.7 trillion US dollars,
a purchasing power parity.

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:40.630
Three of its major economies,
Mexico, Brazil, and Argentina

00:16:40.630 --> 00:16:42.520
are active members of the exclusive

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:44.763
world economic elite G20 group.

00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:47.670
According to a report
and economics and trade

00:16:47.670 --> 00:16:49.110
by the Economic Commission

00:16:49.110 --> 00:16:50.890
for Latin America and
the Caribbean, ECLAC,

00:16:50.890 --> 00:16:53.540
better known in Spanish as CEPAL,

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:56.410
in 2012, the region is predicted to grow

00:16:56.410 --> 00:16:59.560
at 3.1% this last year,

00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:03.413
which is higher than the 2.2%
of world economic growth.

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:07.663
More precisely, the ECLAC
report states that, and I quote,

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:12.160
the region's economic
reforms of past decades,

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:14.680
its fiscal and macroeconomic prudence,

00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:17.170
and it's sound financial supervision,

00:17:17.170 --> 00:17:20.670
together with ever closer
commercial ties with China

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:22.990
and other emerging economies,

00:17:22.990 --> 00:17:26.020
have allowed it not only
to successfully navigate

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:30.350
through the worst international
crisis of the past 80 years,

00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:32.210
but also to enter the new decade

00:17:32.210 --> 00:17:33.870
with a promising outlook for growth

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:36.300
and advances in quality of life.

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:37.830
And for the first time in its history,

00:17:37.830 --> 00:17:40.440
the region achieved during the past decade

00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:44.180
a combination of high-growth,
macroeconomic stability,

00:17:44.180 --> 00:17:47.630
poverty reduction, and improvement
in income distribution.

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:50.250
And with regards to
global economic recovery,

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:53.790
the region is today firmly
part of the solution.

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:56.200
It was not part of the original problem.

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:57.063
End the quote.

00:17:58.370 --> 00:18:02.900
For this year 2013, growth
projection will heavily depend

00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:05.910
on the performance of the global economy.

00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:07.850
At this moment, the economic situation

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:10.570
in the European Union is still gloomy.

00:18:10.570 --> 00:18:12.207
Recession persists in some of

00:18:12.207 --> 00:18:14.477
the larger economies in the region,

00:18:14.477 --> 00:18:16.800
and this will surely have an impact

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:18.750
on the South American countries

00:18:18.750 --> 00:18:21.870
that have Europe as its
major trading partner.

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:26.020
Likewise, some of the emerging
economies like the BRICS

00:18:26.020 --> 00:18:29.140
which includes Brazil, Russia, China,

00:18:29.140 --> 00:18:31.640
India, and now South Africa,

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:33.593
have decelerated their growth.

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:37.440
And all of this will also
influence on the short term

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:40.463
Latin American and Caribbean
economic performance.

00:18:41.780 --> 00:18:45.180
US economy, regardless of
being in a better shape

00:18:45.180 --> 00:18:47.920
than the European Union, is still fragile.

00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:50.224
It has not fully recovered from

00:18:50.224 --> 00:18:51.810
the global economic meltdown.

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:54.410
This has provoked a decrease of US trade

00:18:54.410 --> 00:18:57.090
and investments in the region.

00:18:57.090 --> 00:19:02.090
Imports have dropped from
51% in 2000 to 33% in 2009,

00:19:04.730 --> 00:19:09.533
and exports fell from 60% to
39% during the same period.

00:19:10.690 --> 00:19:15.240
In past decades, President
Kennedy's Alliance for Progress,

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:18.700
President Bush Sr.'s
Initiative for the Americas,

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:21.860
and President Clinton's call
for a Summit of the Americas,

00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:24.210
were all visionary and strategic proposals

00:19:24.210 --> 00:19:28.860
designed to enhance trade and
cooperation in the region.

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:31.160
None of those exists today.

00:19:31.160 --> 00:19:33.610
Hence there is a growing perception

00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:38.233
that the US lacks a strategic
vision towards the region.

00:19:39.140 --> 00:19:41.420
In order to reverse that,

00:19:41.420 --> 00:19:44.560
the US will need a renewed set of policies

00:19:44.560 --> 00:19:46.530
to address the remaining priorities

00:19:46.530 --> 00:19:49.300
of its Latin American
and Caribbean agenda,

00:19:49.300 --> 00:19:53.900
which includes issues of
security, crime violence,

00:19:53.900 --> 00:19:57.990
repatriation of criminals,
illegal drug trafficking,

00:19:57.990 --> 00:20:01.290
illegal weapons trade,
human rights protection,

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:05.763
labor rights, climate change,
remittances, and migration.

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:10.047
Yet the question, does Latin America

00:20:10.047 --> 00:20:13.970
and the Caribbean really
matter, still looms out there.

00:20:13.970 --> 00:20:15.670
And in response, we have to say that

00:20:15.670 --> 00:20:19.003
the region is a place
of enormous contrasts.

00:20:19.960 --> 00:20:23.800
It is not wealthy enough to
become a state-of-the-art center

00:20:23.800 --> 00:20:26.760
for international financial transactions.

00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:30.280
It is not poor enough to
provoke worldwide pity.

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:34.140
It is not dangerous enough
to generate global fear.

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:36.960
So what is the importance
of Latin America,

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:39.173
and where does its major contribution lie?

00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:42.180
For a long time it has been considered

00:20:42.180 --> 00:20:44.040
that it is in the realm of culture

00:20:44.040 --> 00:20:47.040
where Latin America and the
Caribbean have made their mark

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:50.163
and built their brand and
reputation and a global scale.

00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:55.070
It has been through
literature, music, dance, film

00:20:55.070 --> 00:20:57.860
painting, and the arts in general

00:20:57.860 --> 00:21:00.323
where Latin American
soul has been discovered.

00:21:01.510 --> 00:21:04.336
Who has not been touched
by Gabriel Garcia Marquez

00:21:04.336 --> 00:21:07.890
(speaks foreign language)
100 Years of Solitude,

00:21:07.890 --> 00:21:10.110
Mario Vargas Llosa's
(speaks foreign language)

00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:13.000
about the Dominican Republic's
dictatorship Trujillo,

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:14.680
The Feast of the Goat,

00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:18.884
or the poems by the Chilean
Nobel Prize winner Pablo Neruda?

00:21:18.884 --> 00:21:22.477
(speaking foreign language)

00:21:22.477 --> 00:21:25.360
What about admiring the widely
recognized artistic movements

00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:27.880
like muralism, (speaks foreign language)

00:21:27.880 --> 00:21:29.020
which began in Mexico,

00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:32.530
represented by such outstanding
figures as Diego Rivera,

00:21:32.530 --> 00:21:36.410
David Alfaro Siqueiros,
Jose Clemente Orozco?

00:21:36.410 --> 00:21:38.510
Or the paintings of Frida Kahlo,

00:21:38.510 --> 00:21:39.900
one of the most distinguished

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:42.470
Latin American artists of all times,

00:21:42.470 --> 00:21:43.640
who had a style that combined

00:21:43.640 --> 00:21:47.220
realism, symbolism, and surrealism?

00:21:47.220 --> 00:21:49.170
Who has not jumped from his or her seat

00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:51.390
at the south of merengue, salsa,

00:21:51.390 --> 00:21:53.673
bachata, vallenato, or reggaeton?

00:21:54.650 --> 00:21:58.360
Who has not laughed over the
years with Cantinflas movies,

00:21:58.360 --> 00:22:00.850
or sensed the emotions
of Colombian soap operas

00:22:00.850 --> 00:22:03.463
like (speaks foreign language) Ugly Betty?

00:22:04.620 --> 00:22:06.210
In addition to this notorious

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:08.460
cultural presence in the world,

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:09.930
for the last three decades,

00:22:09.930 --> 00:22:12.370
given the enormous demographic, economic,

00:22:12.370 --> 00:22:15.090
social, and political transformations

00:22:15.090 --> 00:22:17.410
that have been taking place in the region,

00:22:17.410 --> 00:22:20.030
Latin America and the
Caribbean has emerged

00:22:20.030 --> 00:22:22.450
as a key player in global affairs,

00:22:22.450 --> 00:22:24.633
one that needs to be watched closely.

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:30.240
Due to the implementation
of sound social policies,

00:22:30.240 --> 00:22:35.240
life expectancy has increased
from 71 years of age in 2000

00:22:36.383 --> 00:22:40.140
to 74 years of age now in 2012.

00:22:40.140 --> 00:22:43.120
Child mortality has
decreased significantly

00:22:43.120 --> 00:22:45.050
in the last 10 years.

00:22:45.050 --> 00:22:50.050
Unemployment has dropped from
9% in 2005 to 6.4% in 2012.

00:22:52.090 --> 00:22:55.290
Poverty and extreme poverty
have fallen as well,

00:22:55.290 --> 00:23:00.290
from 48% in 1990 to 28.8% in 2012.

00:23:01.810 --> 00:23:04.020
All this has resulted in an increase

00:23:04.020 --> 00:23:06.190
of the Human Development Index,

00:23:06.190 --> 00:23:09.350
and expansion of a new more sophisticated

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:11.680
global oriented middle class

00:23:11.680 --> 00:23:14.380
in the different countries in the region.

00:23:14.380 --> 00:23:16.540
Through great efforts
and innovative programs,

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:18.540
illiteracy has been seriously addressed.

00:23:18.540 --> 00:23:20.790
For the first time in
the region's history,

00:23:20.790 --> 00:23:22.960
access to education has broadened,

00:23:22.960 --> 00:23:25.000
making it available to almost everyone

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:27.870
regardless of distance
and social conditions.

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:29.410
Teacher training programs

00:23:29.410 --> 00:23:32.320
and salary increases
have been materialized.

00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:36.520
New technologies have been
introduced at all academic levels

00:23:36.520 --> 00:23:40.140
rising the efficiency, content,
and quality of education

00:23:40.140 --> 00:23:43.053
even though there is still
much to be done in that field.

00:23:44.300 --> 00:23:46.840
In terms of infrastructure development,

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:49.697
the changes in nearly every
country in Latin America

00:23:49.697 --> 00:23:54.010
and the Caribbean have been
astounding and breathtaking.

00:23:54.010 --> 00:23:56.870
The skylines in major
cities have been replaced

00:23:56.870 --> 00:24:00.140
with new buildings constructed
with steel and glass,

00:24:00.140 --> 00:24:03.690
those resembling the modern
view of developed countries.

00:24:03.690 --> 00:24:07.280
That can be fully appreciated
in cities like Sao Paulo,

00:24:07.280 --> 00:24:12.280
Mexico City, Panama City,
Bogota, Santiago de Chile,

00:24:13.490 --> 00:24:16.633
Buenos Aires, and of course Santo Domingo.

00:24:18.580 --> 00:24:21.220
Major improvements have been achieved

00:24:21.220 --> 00:24:24.420
in paving roads, providing fresh water,

00:24:24.420 --> 00:24:27.020
building homes for the
poor and middle class,

00:24:27.020 --> 00:24:31.070
erecting schools and hospitals,
constructing subway systems,

00:24:31.070 --> 00:24:33.120
and promoting access to new information

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:34.933
and communications technologies.

00:24:35.830 --> 00:24:38.200
Internet in Latin
America and the Caribbean

00:24:38.200 --> 00:24:40.180
is in continuous growth.

00:24:40.180 --> 00:24:44.810
At this moment, there are
more than 255 million users,

00:24:44.810 --> 00:24:47.243
which is nearly 1/2 the
region's population.

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:51.200
as the World Bank established
in a recent report,

00:24:51.200 --> 00:24:54.610
the region also leads
in global mobile growth,

00:24:54.610 --> 00:24:59.030
with almost 660 million
mobile telephones in use.

00:24:59.030 --> 00:25:03.330
That represents a density
of 107 cellular devices

00:25:03.330 --> 00:25:04.680
for every 100 residents.

00:25:04.680 --> 00:25:06.320
That means there are
more phones than people

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:07.593
in Latin America today.

00:25:09.400 --> 00:25:11.900
Nowadays, Latin America
and the Caribbean nations

00:25:11.900 --> 00:25:14.660
have become urban, modern societies.

00:25:14.660 --> 00:25:18.980
More than 81% of the
population lives in cities,

00:25:18.980 --> 00:25:21.767
which was exactly the
opposite 50 years ago.

00:25:21.767 --> 00:25:25.050
And many economic
activities have also shifted

00:25:25.050 --> 00:25:28.240
into a more service-oriented economy.

00:25:28.240 --> 00:25:31.010
So there is an undeniable
sense of progress,

00:25:31.010 --> 00:25:33.500
social change, and modernization

00:25:33.500 --> 00:25:36.800
taking place around the
region, and the only question

00:25:36.800 --> 00:25:39.890
or sense of uncertainty
relating to its future

00:25:39.890 --> 00:25:42.333
is about its long-term sustainability.

00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:46.990
In order to become
sustainable and competitive

00:25:46.990 --> 00:25:48.860
in the 21st century,

00:25:48.860 --> 00:25:51.170
the Latin American and
Caribbean nations must be able

00:25:51.170 --> 00:25:53.520
to move forward with an agenda

00:25:53.520 --> 00:25:56.140
to improve the quality of education,

00:25:56.140 --> 00:26:00.440
promote science and
technology, enhance innovation,

00:26:00.440 --> 00:26:04.120
diversify their economies,
increase productivity,

00:26:04.120 --> 00:26:07.073
and integrate within the
global chain of production.

00:26:08.610 --> 00:26:12.053
Moving now towards a more
sensitive area if I may,

00:26:12.940 --> 00:26:17.560
I know that due to President
Hugo Chavez's recent passing,

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:19.750
and upcoming presidential election

00:26:19.750 --> 00:26:22.520
scheduled for next Sunday in Venezuela,

00:26:22.520 --> 00:26:26.010
there is some concern
especially here in the US

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:30.200
about the strength and viability
of democracy in the region,

00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:33.310
and about the political meaning and role

00:26:33.310 --> 00:26:37.863
of what has been identified as
the new Latin American left.

00:26:39.320 --> 00:26:43.990
One must remember that
from some 200 years ago,

00:26:43.990 --> 00:26:47.000
since achieving their independence,

00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:48.740
the Latin American and Caribbean nations

00:26:48.740 --> 00:26:51.280
always aspired to install democracy

00:26:51.280 --> 00:26:53.990
within their respective societies.

00:26:53.990 --> 00:26:56.490
But for different historical reasons,

00:26:56.490 --> 00:26:58.530
with only very few exceptions,

00:26:58.530 --> 00:27:01.090
these aspirations were met fulfilled

00:27:01.090 --> 00:27:04.223
during the 19th for the first
1/2 of the 20th century.

00:27:05.780 --> 00:27:09.570
It was only after the end
of the Second World War

00:27:09.570 --> 00:27:12.370
that a swing of the pendulum
in favor of democracy

00:27:12.370 --> 00:27:14.030
began to take place in countries

00:27:14.030 --> 00:27:16.483
like Guatemala and Venezuela.

00:27:17.320 --> 00:27:21.470
However, soon this swing began to reverse.

00:27:21.470 --> 00:27:24.540
In Guatemala, Jacobo Arbenz nationalized

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:28.260
the United Fruit Company
an American corporation,

00:27:28.260 --> 00:27:30.770
and soon after, the US
government intervened,

00:27:30.770 --> 00:27:34.153
toppling Arbenz on the grounds
of being pro-communist.

00:27:35.490 --> 00:27:38.630
The Cold War influence and
blurring the differences

00:27:38.630 --> 00:27:40.870
among US government officials

00:27:40.870 --> 00:27:44.580
between nationalistic
policies and communism.

00:27:44.580 --> 00:27:47.370
The Truman Doctrine or
Policy of Containment

00:27:47.370 --> 00:27:50.510
clouded the vision of
various US administrations

00:27:50.510 --> 00:27:53.180
in dealing with the issue of nationalism

00:27:53.180 --> 00:27:57.670
and not making the mistake of
confusing it with communism.

00:27:57.670 --> 00:28:00.130
Even Fidel Castro in 1952

00:28:00.130 --> 00:28:03.820
was running to become a member
of the Chamber of Deputies,

00:28:03.820 --> 00:28:06.450
representing the province of Havana

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:08.970
when Fulgencio Batista led the coup

00:28:08.970 --> 00:28:11.783
that derailed Cuba from
its democratic path.

00:28:13.080 --> 00:28:17.970
During its first stage after
1959, the Cuban Revolution

00:28:17.970 --> 00:28:22.530
was not a socialist revolution,
but a Nationalist one.

00:28:22.530 --> 00:28:27.140
It appeals not to Karl Marx
or to Vladimir Ilyich Lenin,

00:28:27.140 --> 00:28:31.393
but more to Jose Marti, Antonio
Maceo, and Maximo Gomez.

00:28:32.290 --> 00:28:36.050
But Castro had nationalized,
as Arbenz did before him,

00:28:36.050 --> 00:28:38.310
American corporate interests.

00:28:38.310 --> 00:28:41.780
And as a reaction to that,
the Invasion of Pigs,

00:28:41.780 --> 00:28:45.960
which was oriented towards
overthrowing his regime, failed.

00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:50.630
It was only after that episode in 1961

00:28:50.630 --> 00:28:53.260
that the Cuban Revolution aligned itself

00:28:53.260 --> 00:28:54.230
with the Soviet Union,

00:28:54.230 --> 00:28:57.863
thus becoming a major player
in the Cold War drama.

00:28:58.830 --> 00:29:01.300
My own country the Dominican Republic

00:29:01.300 --> 00:29:03.830
is a clear historical example of confusion

00:29:03.830 --> 00:29:06.280
created among US policymakers

00:29:06.280 --> 00:29:08.840
between nationalism and communism

00:29:08.840 --> 00:29:11.770
when President Johnson
ordered a military occupation

00:29:11.770 --> 00:29:13.690
of the small island nation

00:29:13.690 --> 00:29:16.730
to prevent it from becoming a second Cuba.

00:29:16.730 --> 00:29:18.930
What really was at stake
in the Dominican Republic

00:29:18.930 --> 00:29:21.540
in 1965 had nothing to do with

00:29:21.540 --> 00:29:23.660
a country becoming a second Cuba,

00:29:23.660 --> 00:29:25.520
it was about the return to democracy

00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:27.300
and constitutional order,

00:29:27.300 --> 00:29:30.310
and the reposition of Juan
Bosch to the presidency,

00:29:30.310 --> 00:29:34.760
who had been overthrown by a
military coup d'etat in 1963,

00:29:34.760 --> 00:29:37.410
after winning in a landslide

00:29:37.410 --> 00:29:40.000
the first democratic
elections that took place

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:43.730
in the country after the fall
of the Trujillo dictatorship.

00:29:43.730 --> 00:29:47.710
But exactly the opposite
occurred in neighboring Haiti,

00:29:47.710 --> 00:29:51.850
where President Clinton,
authorized by UN Council resolution

00:29:51.850 --> 00:29:54.120
ordered the deployment of US troops

00:29:54.120 --> 00:29:55.790
to remove the military regime

00:29:55.790 --> 00:29:59.280
installed in 1991 by a coup d'etat

00:29:59.280 --> 00:30:03.510
and restore the elected
president Jean-Bertrand Aristide

00:30:03.510 --> 00:30:05.910
back into office to end his term.

00:30:05.910 --> 00:30:08.130
What made the difference in terms of

00:30:08.130 --> 00:30:11.270
decision making in these similar cases

00:30:11.270 --> 00:30:13.780
between the Dominican Republic and Haiti

00:30:13.780 --> 00:30:16.260
was one main and single factor.

00:30:16.260 --> 00:30:18.810
The Cold War, which divided the world

00:30:18.810 --> 00:30:22.180
in two different lines of
thought and political behavior.

00:30:22.180 --> 00:30:24.440
With the end of that historical period,

00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:26.070
the Red Scare disappeared,

00:30:26.070 --> 00:30:28.760
and with it the fear
that a communist takeover

00:30:28.760 --> 00:30:32.040
might deviate any
democratic political process

00:30:33.610 --> 00:30:35.820
It has been only during
the last three decades

00:30:35.820 --> 00:30:37.810
that democracy has been fully established

00:30:37.810 --> 00:30:39.930
in Latin America and the Caribbean.

00:30:39.930 --> 00:30:41.150
According to recent polls

00:30:41.150 --> 00:30:45.090
taken by a highly recognized
firm Latinobarometro,

00:30:45.090 --> 00:30:48.490
at this moment there is a high
appreciation in the region

00:30:48.490 --> 00:30:51.550
of what democracy has
brought to different nations

00:30:51.550 --> 00:30:53.760
in terms of respect for human rights,

00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:56.970
free and fair elections,
freedom of the press,

00:30:56.970 --> 00:30:59.070
independence of the judiciary,

00:30:59.070 --> 00:31:00.830
the respect for the rule of law,

00:31:00.830 --> 00:31:02.563
and government accountability.

00:31:03.740 --> 00:31:06.110
With the flourishing of
democracy in the region,

00:31:06.110 --> 00:31:08.340
organizations that were
originally involved

00:31:08.340 --> 00:31:10.500
in armed revolutionary struggle

00:31:10.500 --> 00:31:13.410
have put out their arms
and integrated themselves

00:31:13.410 --> 00:31:15.380
within the democratic process

00:31:15.380 --> 00:31:18.300
to participate through
the electoral system

00:31:18.300 --> 00:31:21.040
as a way of having access to power.

00:31:21.040 --> 00:31:23.740
This is the case, just to
mention a few examples,

00:31:23.740 --> 00:31:27.783
of the (speaks foreign
language) in El Salvador,

00:31:28.647 --> 00:31:31.966
(speaks foreign language) in Nicaragua,

00:31:31.966 --> 00:31:33.610
(speaks foreign language) in Uruguay,

00:31:33.610 --> 00:31:35.320
all of which after many years

00:31:35.320 --> 00:31:37.640
of political arms confrontation,

00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:40.860
have won national elections
in a fairly manner

00:31:40.860 --> 00:31:43.763
and now rule their countries
through Democratic means.

00:31:44.610 --> 00:31:47.900
During the Cold War,
the left-wing movement

00:31:47.900 --> 00:31:50.930
in Latin America or any
other part of the world

00:31:50.930 --> 00:31:52.240
was engaged in a process

00:31:52.240 --> 00:31:55.490
of bringing down capitalism as a system.

00:31:55.490 --> 00:31:58.320
This is not the case
in Latin America today.

00:31:58.320 --> 00:32:00.510
No elected government in the region

00:32:00.510 --> 00:32:02.407
is evenly remotely considering

00:32:02.407 --> 00:32:05.340
overturning the capitalist system.

00:32:05.340 --> 00:32:08.570
Nowadays, left-wing
politics in Latin America

00:32:08.570 --> 00:32:11.500
is more concerned with
challenging what is called

00:32:11.500 --> 00:32:14.270
the orthodox free-market philosophy,

00:32:14.270 --> 00:32:16.800
identified as the Washington Consensus,

00:32:16.800 --> 00:32:20.383
and the flaws and disparities
emerging from globalization.

00:32:21.560 --> 00:32:24.660
The Washington Consensus
was essentially a response

00:32:24.660 --> 00:32:26.970
to the macroeconomic crisis that affected

00:32:26.970 --> 00:32:29.840
Latin America and the
Caribbean in the 80s.

00:32:29.840 --> 00:32:32.660
It focused mainly on market stabilization,

00:32:32.660 --> 00:32:35.600
trade, and privatization,
and ignored the social issues

00:32:35.600 --> 00:32:39.340
of poverty, unemployment,
and social inequality.

00:32:39.340 --> 00:32:41.810
This lack of human or social element,

00:32:41.810 --> 00:32:45.310
labeled as a market-centered
neoliberal approach,

00:32:45.310 --> 00:32:47.790
generated the conditions
for its rejection in

00:32:47.790 --> 00:32:49.410
different parts of the region,

00:32:49.410 --> 00:32:51.680
creating a breeding ground for the return

00:32:51.680 --> 00:32:54.960
of state-centric policies
in Latin America.

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:58.420
With different characteristics,
national conditions,

00:32:58.420 --> 00:33:02.300
international styles, and
personal leadership styles,

00:33:02.300 --> 00:33:05.170
it is this view of economic
and social development,

00:33:05.170 --> 00:33:07.930
centered under state
instead of the market,

00:33:07.930 --> 00:33:10.600
which has been considered
as a swing to the left

00:33:10.600 --> 00:33:12.393
in Latin America and the Caribbean.

00:33:13.500 --> 00:33:16.830
Other elements that have also
contributed to these images

00:33:16.830 --> 00:33:18.920
of Latin America leading towards the left

00:33:18.920 --> 00:33:22.340
have been the fact that
some elected heads of state

00:33:22.340 --> 00:33:26.580
come from the indigenous
movement, the labor unions,

00:33:26.580 --> 00:33:29.830
the progressive military,
or feminist groups.

00:33:29.830 --> 00:33:34.780
Such as Evo Morales, Luiz
Inacio da Silva Lula,

00:33:34.780 --> 00:33:38.650
Dilma Rousseff, and Cristina
Fernandez de Kirchner.

00:33:38.650 --> 00:33:41.340
Therefore at this pivotal moment,

00:33:41.340 --> 00:33:44.680
more than the left as a dominant force,

00:33:44.680 --> 00:33:47.500
it is fair to indicate that two opposing

00:33:47.500 --> 00:33:50.680
economic, political, and social views

00:33:50.680 --> 00:33:53.470
pervade Latin America in the Caribbean.

00:33:53.470 --> 00:33:56.413
Neoliberalism and Neopopulism.

00:33:58.130 --> 00:34:00.660
The future of democracy in the region

00:34:00.660 --> 00:34:04.388
will depend upon our ability to overcome

00:34:04.388 --> 00:34:07.513
these two distinct and
contrasting paradigms.

00:34:08.630 --> 00:34:13.020
Instead of being too much
focused on the left or the right

00:34:13.020 --> 00:34:15.270
what the region needs
is a new set of policies

00:34:15.270 --> 00:34:19.270
that can balance the role of
state with that of the market.

00:34:19.270 --> 00:34:21.887
What is also needed at this
moment in Latin America

00:34:21.887 --> 00:34:26.010
and the Caribbean is the capacity
to integrate market forces

00:34:26.010 --> 00:34:28.610
with social policies upheld by a culture

00:34:28.610 --> 00:34:32.963
of solidarity, corporation, and
partnership for development.

00:34:34.042 --> 00:34:37.620
At the beginning of this second
decade of the 21st century,

00:34:37.620 --> 00:34:40.800
what Latin America needs is
more and better democracy,

00:34:40.800 --> 00:34:43.290
more sustainable economic growth,

00:34:43.290 --> 00:34:46.230
preservation of the environment
and natural resources,

00:34:46.230 --> 00:34:49.580
more jobs, better quality
education and health care,

00:34:49.580 --> 00:34:53.083
and widespread prosperity
and opportunity for all.

00:34:54.170 --> 00:34:56.410
If you allow me, I would like to conclude

00:34:56.410 --> 00:35:00.473
by briefly referring to the
Latino community in the US.

00:35:01.660 --> 00:35:04.430
And as indicated by the Census Bureau,

00:35:04.430 --> 00:35:07.890
as of 2012 there are 52 million Latinos

00:35:07.890 --> 00:35:10.100
living in this great nation,

00:35:10.100 --> 00:35:11.810
making it the largest ethnic

00:35:11.810 --> 00:35:13.933
or race minority at this moment.

00:35:14.900 --> 00:35:19.900
They constitute 16.7% of
the US total population,

00:35:20.200 --> 00:35:23.300
and the projections are that by 2050

00:35:23.300 --> 00:35:26.510
will account for 132 million inhabitants

00:35:26.510 --> 00:35:30.623
representing 30% of US total population.

00:35:31.470 --> 00:35:34.330
Latinos will continue
entering the workforce

00:35:34.330 --> 00:35:35.940
in growing numbers.

00:35:35.940 --> 00:35:40.940
In 2011, it reached 15% of
the total US labor force,

00:35:41.767 --> 00:35:46.403
and by 2050 it is expected
to be at the level of 24%.

00:35:47.490 --> 00:35:52.490
In the last 15 years, there
has been a 53% increase

00:35:52.530 --> 00:35:56.410
of the total number of Latinos
serving in elected office,

00:35:56.410 --> 00:35:58.370
which demonstrates their growing political

00:35:58.370 --> 00:36:00.840
influence at the government level.

00:36:00.840 --> 00:36:03.510
Many Latinos have distinguished themselves

00:36:03.510 --> 00:36:06.870
in the arts, academia, business, politics,

00:36:06.870 --> 00:36:09.120
and diverse professional activities.

00:36:09.120 --> 00:36:13.080
Names such as Jennifer
Lopez, better known as JLo,

00:36:13.080 --> 00:36:17.120
Marc Anthony, Carlos
Santana, Gloria Estefan,

00:36:17.120 --> 00:36:19.253
Salma Hayek, Zoe Saldana,

00:36:20.800 --> 00:36:22.880
A-Rod, his real name is Alex Rodriguez,

00:36:22.880 --> 00:36:24.940
but he changed it for A-Rod,

00:36:24.940 --> 00:36:27.690
and Junot Diaz, just to name a few,

00:36:27.690 --> 00:36:31.582
are very familiar and quite
popular around the nation,

00:36:31.582 --> 00:36:35.513
which makes us all Latin
Americans very proud of them.

00:36:36.840 --> 00:36:40.380
The Latino community represents
a great force in the US,

00:36:40.380 --> 00:36:43.330
as can be proven with
the fact that they have

00:36:43.330 --> 00:36:47.150
a purchasing power of 1.5
trillion dollars a year

00:36:47.150 --> 00:36:49.100
within the US market,

00:36:49.100 --> 00:36:50.260
but also through the results

00:36:50.260 --> 00:36:52.470
of the last presidential elections,

00:36:52.470 --> 00:36:55.970
in which it became
decisive in the outcome.

00:36:55.970 --> 00:36:58.050
Latinos made their mark an election day

00:36:58.050 --> 00:37:01.370
as they voted for President
Barack Obama's reelection

00:37:01.370 --> 00:37:06.370
over Republican candidate
Mitt Romney 71% to 27%.

00:37:07.160 --> 00:37:10.070
And they were instrumental in
helping President Obama win

00:37:10.070 --> 00:37:12.330
in two of the key battleground states

00:37:12.330 --> 00:37:17.330
Nevada 70% to 25% and Colorado 75% to 23%.

00:37:19.210 --> 00:37:22.004
In Spanish we say Obama
(speaks foreign language).

00:37:22.004 --> 00:37:23.660
(audience laughing)

00:37:23.660 --> 00:37:26.910
Taking into account the electoral results,

00:37:26.910 --> 00:37:29.663
Republican strategy and CNN
contributor Ana Navarro,

00:37:30.839 --> 00:37:32.320
who was the National Latino co-chair

00:37:32.320 --> 00:37:35.857
of McCain's presidential
campaign stated, quote,

00:37:35.857 --> 00:37:37.867
"if we don't do better with Hispanics,

00:37:37.867 --> 00:37:40.780
"we'll be out of the White House forever."

00:37:40.780 --> 00:37:43.560
Noting the importance of Latinos,

00:37:43.560 --> 00:37:46.087
President Obama said, quote,

00:37:46.087 --> 00:37:49.427
"our country was built on
and continues to thrive

00:37:49.427 --> 00:37:52.197
"on its diversity, and there is no doubt

00:37:52.197 --> 00:37:56.117
"that the future of the United
States is inextricably linked

00:37:56.117 --> 00:37:58.527
"to the future of the Hispanic community."

00:37:59.590 --> 00:38:03.290
Beyond question, Latin
America's influence has expanded

00:38:03.290 --> 00:38:06.430
at such length around the globe

00:38:06.430 --> 00:38:10.120
that it has recently reached
the unreachable dream,

00:38:10.120 --> 00:38:13.840
a Latin American Pope, in Papa Francisco

00:38:13.840 --> 00:38:15.840
sitting on the chair of Saint Peter

00:38:15.840 --> 00:38:17.030
in the heart of the Vatican.

00:38:17.030 --> 00:38:18.553
Thank you very much.

00:38:18.553 --> 00:38:21.720
(audience applauding)

00:38:43.530 --> 00:38:45.680
- [Jeff] If you'd like to ask a question,

00:38:45.680 --> 00:38:47.600
we've got some students with microphones.

00:38:47.600 --> 00:38:50.160
Just raise your hand, the
president will call on you,

00:38:50.160 --> 00:38:53.007
and wait for a microphone to get to you.

00:38:56.349 --> 00:38:58.516
(mumbles)

00:39:01.681 --> 00:39:04.060
It's an unusually shy
(Leonel laughing)

00:39:04.060 --> 00:39:04.893
audience.

00:39:06.330 --> 00:39:09.605
- Either everybody was convinced
or I bored everybody here.

00:39:09.605 --> 00:39:12.272
(both laughing)

00:39:14.010 --> 00:39:15.280
- [Man] Mr. President, I have a question

00:39:15.280 --> 00:39:18.310
that is related to last night's talk,

00:39:18.310 --> 00:39:21.960
which was aimed at more
the undergraduate students

00:39:21.960 --> 00:39:23.103
in Timothy Dwight.

00:39:24.350 --> 00:39:28.640
You talked about your college experience

00:39:28.640 --> 00:39:30.670
in Dominican Republic,

00:39:30.670 --> 00:39:35.670
and it was more or less based
on a political affiliation.

00:39:38.010 --> 00:39:39.540
Can you elaborate on

00:39:39.540 --> 00:39:43.533
how that plays out for 18 or 20 year old,

00:39:44.810 --> 00:39:47.340
versus the way the average Yalie

00:39:49.070 --> 00:39:51.053
or the average America goes to college?

00:39:53.750 --> 00:39:58.100
I was here the 70s, and it
was more like that then here,

00:39:58.100 --> 00:40:00.790
but now it doesn't seem to be particularly

00:40:00.790 --> 00:40:03.890
tied to your political beliefs,

00:40:03.890 --> 00:40:07.170
your road through college,
it's not that big a deal.

00:40:07.170 --> 00:40:10.860
But obviously it is, it was in your day

00:40:10.860 --> 00:40:12.914
in the Dominican Republic.

00:40:12.914 --> 00:40:15.220
- Well there is a conventional wisdom that

00:40:15.220 --> 00:40:18.490
you tend to learn only
through academic institutions,

00:40:18.490 --> 00:40:21.310
and even though you get an opportunity

00:40:21.310 --> 00:40:23.900
to really grow intellectually
and professionally

00:40:23.900 --> 00:40:25.870
through academic institutions,

00:40:25.870 --> 00:40:27.770
there are other ways of learning too

00:40:27.770 --> 00:40:29.840
beyond academic institutions.

00:40:29.840 --> 00:40:31.890
And it was the case in
the Dominican Republic

00:40:31.890 --> 00:40:35.600
when I returned at the end
of the 60s beginning the 70s,

00:40:35.600 --> 00:40:38.890
and it had to do with national conditions

00:40:38.890 --> 00:40:41.070
and what was taking
place around the world.

00:40:41.070 --> 00:40:44.040
The people of my generation
became very interested,

00:40:44.040 --> 00:40:47.233
for example, in what
was going on in Vietnam,

00:40:48.810 --> 00:40:51.780
US policy towards Latin
America and the Caribbean,

00:40:51.780 --> 00:40:54.240
the consequences of the
US military occupation

00:40:54.240 --> 00:40:56.560
in the Dominican Republic in '65.

00:40:56.560 --> 00:41:00.470
So there was a generation that
was intellectually curious

00:41:00.470 --> 00:41:03.760
and that needed to participate
actively in politics

00:41:03.760 --> 00:41:07.820
in order to contribute to a
social change in our country.

00:41:07.820 --> 00:41:09.970
So that created an
intellectual environment

00:41:09.970 --> 00:41:11.990
even at the neighborhood level

00:41:11.990 --> 00:41:15.110
where college students were self-taught.

00:41:15.110 --> 00:41:17.580
They were reading things that
were not assigned in school,

00:41:17.580 --> 00:41:19.850
but it's just because
they were were interested

00:41:19.850 --> 00:41:23.200
and they thought this
would be intellectual tools

00:41:23.200 --> 00:41:25.270
for opening up their
minds and understanding

00:41:25.270 --> 00:41:27.070
what was going on in the world.

00:41:27.070 --> 00:41:30.410
And so I when I returned
to the Dominican Republic,

00:41:30.410 --> 00:41:33.600
I was caught up within that political

00:41:33.600 --> 00:41:35.540
and environment situation,

00:41:35.540 --> 00:41:40.540
and I think it somehow influenced
me in my world outlook.

00:41:41.460 --> 00:41:43.360
Now of course, there's another factor.

00:41:44.460 --> 00:41:47.530
I mentioned Juan Bosch,
a former president,

00:41:47.530 --> 00:41:49.460
and the most prolific and prestigious

00:41:49.460 --> 00:41:51.900
Dominican intellectual of all times.

00:41:51.900 --> 00:41:54.670
He returned from exile
at the beginning of 1970

00:41:54.670 --> 00:41:58.110
and began having daily lectures by radio,

00:41:58.110 --> 00:42:01.710
a little bit like FDR here
during the Great Depression.

00:42:01.710 --> 00:42:04.430
But he was a great
lecturer a great thinker,

00:42:04.430 --> 00:42:09.430
and was also very influential
in shaping my generation.

00:42:10.280 --> 00:42:12.900
So in him we found a mentor.

00:42:12.900 --> 00:42:15.880
First very remote I mean he was already

00:42:15.880 --> 00:42:18.510
a well-known Latin American intellectual

00:42:18.510 --> 00:42:21.000
so it was very difficult
to have access to him,

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:22.910
but later on we did, and we established

00:42:22.910 --> 00:42:25.260
a personal relationship with him.

00:42:25.260 --> 00:42:28.705
And we learned reading
his books and we learned

00:42:28.705 --> 00:42:32.720
listening to his lectures and
through his leadership styles.

00:42:32.720 --> 00:42:36.210
So there was also a
mentoring from a great figure

00:42:36.210 --> 00:42:38.790
that gave us a sense of orientation,

00:42:38.790 --> 00:42:40.870
because we were reading many things,

00:42:40.870 --> 00:42:44.750
but they didn't have like
a goal or an objective.

00:42:44.750 --> 00:42:48.400
Now when Juan Bosch came
and he made us become aware

00:42:48.400 --> 00:42:50.420
of the wealth of our national culture

00:42:50.420 --> 00:42:51.770
or our national history

00:42:51.770 --> 00:42:54.270
and the need to move forward
and establish democracy,

00:42:54.270 --> 00:42:55.270
well then that gave us

00:42:55.270 --> 00:42:58.370
a sense of orientation,
a sense of direction.

00:42:58.370 --> 00:43:01.290
And that's how I really got involved,

00:43:01.290 --> 00:43:04.730
participating through politics
and learning through politics

00:43:04.730 --> 00:43:07.100
a lot of different ideas
and different things.

00:43:07.100 --> 00:43:10.620
Because of perhaps the
idea that one has is

00:43:10.620 --> 00:43:13.450
being involved in politics
is only a struggle for power.

00:43:13.450 --> 00:43:14.283
It is not true.

00:43:14.283 --> 00:43:16.740
Through politics you develop many skills

00:43:16.740 --> 00:43:18.970
that otherwise you wouldn't have it.

00:43:18.970 --> 00:43:22.660
Public speaking skills,
organizational skills,

00:43:22.660 --> 00:43:24.610
influencing people, mobilizing people.

00:43:24.610 --> 00:43:27.530
You only learn that
because you participate,

00:43:27.530 --> 00:43:29.580
and through participation
there's a learning curve

00:43:29.580 --> 00:43:31.870
that will transform you as an individual

00:43:31.870 --> 00:43:34.970
to become really influential
within your society,

00:43:34.970 --> 00:43:37.120
within your neighborhood,
within your peers.

00:43:37.120 --> 00:43:38.290
And I think this is the experience

00:43:38.290 --> 00:43:40.280
that I had in the Dominican Republic.

00:43:40.280 --> 00:43:41.920
Another thing one learns is

00:43:41.920 --> 00:43:44.630
everybody has a mentor
at some point in life,

00:43:44.630 --> 00:43:47.260
and later on you become
a mentor to someone else.

00:43:47.260 --> 00:43:49.753
So there's like a chain of events there.

00:43:51.858 --> 00:43:52.691
- [Jeff] I'd like to exercise

00:43:52.691 --> 00:43:54.460
the chair's privilege to ask one question.

00:43:54.460 --> 00:43:55.293
- Yes. (laughs)

00:43:55.293 --> 00:43:58.490
- Which is you presented
a compelling I thought

00:43:59.990 --> 00:44:04.630
array of factors on
which the region has made

00:44:04.630 --> 00:44:08.220
consistent and considerable progress

00:44:08.220 --> 00:44:09.893
over the past two decades.

00:44:11.210 --> 00:44:14.300
Democratization, economic growth,

00:44:14.300 --> 00:44:17.343
even relative to other parts of the world,

00:44:18.320 --> 00:44:23.320
reduction of poverty,
diversification of economies,

00:44:23.350 --> 00:44:25.560
even the growth of the Latino population

00:44:25.560 --> 00:44:28.490
of the United States and the
implications that that has

00:44:28.490 --> 00:44:32.950
for perhaps a greater region here.

00:44:32.950 --> 00:44:34.900
What's your center of anxiety?

00:44:34.900 --> 00:44:39.410
What do you see as perhaps
the single greatest challenge,

00:44:39.410 --> 00:44:42.810
or the thing that you
would worry about most

00:44:42.810 --> 00:44:47.810
that could stop or reverse
some of that progress?

00:44:50.130 --> 00:44:52.710
- This is interesting because
we have been celebrating

00:44:52.710 --> 00:44:55.990
since 2008 in Latin
America and the Caribbean

00:44:55.990 --> 00:44:59.060
the 200th anniversary of independence.

00:44:59.060 --> 00:45:01.540
And as I said, right from its beginnings,

00:45:01.540 --> 00:45:04.590
there was an aspiration in in the region

00:45:04.590 --> 00:45:08.150
to install democracy and
promote economic growth

00:45:08.150 --> 00:45:09.130
and create opportunities.

00:45:09.130 --> 00:45:10.700
It never really happened,

00:45:10.700 --> 00:45:13.770
with the exceptions of a few countries.

00:45:13.770 --> 00:45:17.840
So what really has taken place
in the last 30 plus years

00:45:17.840 --> 00:45:21.120
to generate this political, economic,

00:45:21.120 --> 00:45:24.050
and social transformation
in Latin America?

00:45:24.050 --> 00:45:25.330
You'll be a mistake to look at it

00:45:25.330 --> 00:45:28.070
only from a Latin American perspective.

00:45:28.070 --> 00:45:29.690
I think it has to do with everything

00:45:29.690 --> 00:45:32.500
that has been taking place in the world.

00:45:32.500 --> 00:45:33.870
The democratization process

00:45:33.870 --> 00:45:35.740
didn't even begin in Latin America.

00:45:35.740 --> 00:45:38.760
It began in in southern
Europe at the end of the 70s.

00:45:38.760 --> 00:45:43.760
It came with the collapse of
the Salazar regime in Portugal,

00:45:44.140 --> 00:45:47.600
with the end of Francisco
Franco's regime in Spain,

00:45:47.600 --> 00:45:50.680
or the military in Greece.

00:45:50.680 --> 00:45:54.790
Many Latin American
politicians and intellectuals

00:45:54.790 --> 00:45:56.253
were living in Europe at that time

00:45:56.253 --> 00:45:58.700
that were influenced by these events,

00:45:58.700 --> 00:46:02.680
and so when they came back
to their respective countries

00:46:02.680 --> 00:46:06.030
they changed their political strategy,

00:46:06.030 --> 00:46:07.750
which at that time was

00:46:07.750 --> 00:46:11.350
confronting dictatorship with socialism.

00:46:11.350 --> 00:46:15.570
And then they figured out
that it was more difficult

00:46:15.570 --> 00:46:17.560
to confront authoritarian regimes

00:46:17.560 --> 00:46:22.560
presenting an alternative to the system.

00:46:22.800 --> 00:46:24.700
And instead they said, instead of

00:46:24.700 --> 00:46:27.910
of dictatorship versus socialism,

00:46:27.910 --> 00:46:31.600
or as they framed it at the
time, fascism versus socialism,

00:46:31.600 --> 00:46:34.440
it was more dictatorship versus democracy.

00:46:34.440 --> 00:46:37.380
So there was a there was
a change in the mindset

00:46:37.380 --> 00:46:40.770
in Latin American
intellectuals and politicians

00:46:40.770 --> 00:46:43.980
for the first time accepting
democracy in modern times.

00:46:43.980 --> 00:46:47.910
Because democracy was
disdained at some point

00:46:47.910 --> 00:46:51.690
because every time there was
an attempt to install democracy

00:46:51.690 --> 00:46:53.520
there would be a military coup d'etat,

00:46:53.520 --> 00:46:55.730
And perhaps US government officials

00:46:55.730 --> 00:46:57.710
aligned themselves with the new dictators.

00:46:57.710 --> 00:46:59.670
There was a lot of frustration

00:46:59.670 --> 00:47:01.560
with democracy in Latin America,

00:47:01.560 --> 00:47:04.300
and some would say bourgeois democracy,

00:47:04.300 --> 00:47:07.110
or you know (speaks foreign language)

00:47:07.110 --> 00:47:09.880
that mentioned representative system.

00:47:09.880 --> 00:47:12.430
But after that, after what
happened in southern Europe,

00:47:12.430 --> 00:47:14.160
there was a new mindset.

00:47:14.160 --> 00:47:17.270
With that, I think there
are two other factors

00:47:17.270 --> 00:47:21.580
that play an important
influence in this transformation

00:47:24.510 --> 00:47:26.550
from dictatorship to democracy.

00:47:26.550 --> 00:47:29.400
There's a man that I think has
not been given enough credit

00:47:29.400 --> 00:47:33.350
for everything he did in
allowing this transition

00:47:33.350 --> 00:47:34.377
to democracy in Latin America,

00:47:34.377 --> 00:47:36.163
and that is President Jimmy Carter.

00:47:37.270 --> 00:47:39.820
With his human rights policy,

00:47:39.820 --> 00:47:41.750
and pressing for free and fair elections

00:47:41.750 --> 00:47:43.510
at the end of the 70s,

00:47:43.510 --> 00:47:46.300
Jimmy Carter really made
an important contribution

00:47:46.300 --> 00:47:48.670
to political change in Latin America.

00:47:48.670 --> 00:47:51.330
The other have to do with
social democracy in Europe.

00:47:51.330 --> 00:47:54.150
They have an organization
called socialist international,

00:47:54.150 --> 00:47:58.110
which is not Marxist, is
more democratic oriented,

00:47:58.110 --> 00:47:59.860
and they had an opening for Latin America,

00:47:59.860 --> 00:48:02.760
and then traditional populist
Latin American parties

00:48:02.760 --> 00:48:06.520
became part of this organization.

00:48:06.520 --> 00:48:09.080
And so coming from Europe
and coming from the US,

00:48:09.080 --> 00:48:12.710
there was a lot of pressure to organize

00:48:12.710 --> 00:48:15.170
democratic elections,
free and fair elections.

00:48:15.170 --> 00:48:18.490
And beginning in the
Dominican Republic in 1978,

00:48:18.490 --> 00:48:21.290
there has been a third
wave of democratization

00:48:21.290 --> 00:48:22.610
after the Second World War,

00:48:22.610 --> 00:48:24.520
and for the first time, there has been

00:48:24.520 --> 00:48:26.890
no reverse in this situation.

00:48:26.890 --> 00:48:29.040
So in Latin America we all think

00:48:29.040 --> 00:48:31.640
that democracy has come to stay,

00:48:31.640 --> 00:48:35.420
and that we are more now
consolidating democracy,

00:48:35.420 --> 00:48:37.540
consolidating democracy and working on

00:48:37.540 --> 00:48:40.430
how government can be more efficient,

00:48:40.430 --> 00:48:42.240
how can it be more transparent,

00:48:42.240 --> 00:48:44.270
how can it be more accountable,

00:48:44.270 --> 00:48:47.280
how can it deliver what
the people is expecting

00:48:47.280 --> 00:48:49.980
from a government than ever before,

00:48:49.980 --> 00:48:51.900
this never took place before.

00:48:51.900 --> 00:48:55.480
So having resolved the
political question of stability,

00:48:55.480 --> 00:48:57.270
of how do you have access to power,

00:48:57.270 --> 00:48:59.940
you can even consider what I
said in my previous remarks.

00:48:59.940 --> 00:49:03.960
Organizations like
(speaks foreign language)

00:49:03.960 --> 00:49:07.610
which came to power in 1979
through an armed struggle,

00:49:07.610 --> 00:49:09.160
lost elections 10 years later,

00:49:09.160 --> 00:49:12.170
and came back to power again
through electoral means.

00:49:12.170 --> 00:49:14.710
So everybody has accepted
the rules of the game

00:49:14.710 --> 00:49:16.530
that it is through free and fair elections

00:49:16.530 --> 00:49:18.230
that you have access to power.

00:49:18.230 --> 00:49:19.980
Now for the population,

00:49:19.980 --> 00:49:23.040
that is a great
achievement, but not enough,

00:49:23.040 --> 00:49:25.010
because at the end of the game,

00:49:25.010 --> 00:49:28.870
at the end of what you
are making as a proposal,

00:49:28.870 --> 00:49:32.820
what is the economic and
social evolution of society?

00:49:32.820 --> 00:49:35.163
And there have been many reforms.

00:49:36.230 --> 00:49:39.400
The Washington Consensus
I think has a merit

00:49:39.400 --> 00:49:42.270
in that it corrected some of the problems

00:49:42.270 --> 00:49:45.020
of the previous economic
model which is more Keynesian.

00:49:45.878 --> 00:49:50.878
In the sense that it emphasized
macroeconomic stability,

00:49:52.100 --> 00:49:54.970
it emphasized fiscal deficits correction,

00:49:54.970 --> 00:49:56.230
combating hyperinflation.

00:49:56.230 --> 00:49:59.160
So all these things were
good, but still not enough,

00:49:59.160 --> 00:50:00.710
because they didn't have an impact

00:50:00.710 --> 00:50:02.690
in the well-being of the population.

00:50:02.690 --> 00:50:06.450
So what has been questioned
on the Washington Consensus,

00:50:06.450 --> 00:50:09.210
it is that it's too technocratic,

00:50:09.210 --> 00:50:12.600
and it forgot what
policymaking is all about

00:50:12.600 --> 00:50:17.190
is having people as as
the targeted element

00:50:17.190 --> 00:50:19.390
to which you have to influence.

00:50:19.390 --> 00:50:22.060
So going beyond the Washington Consensus,

00:50:22.060 --> 00:50:23.710
the new governments in Latin America

00:50:23.710 --> 00:50:25.950
have installed social policies.

00:50:25.950 --> 00:50:27.940
The whole idea that if the economy grows

00:50:27.940 --> 00:50:29.530
it will trickle down and have an impact

00:50:29.530 --> 00:50:31.580
is being rejected in Latin America,

00:50:31.580 --> 00:50:34.990
and you have to combine
solid macroeconomic policies,

00:50:34.990 --> 00:50:38.380
economic growth, with
active social policies

00:50:38.380 --> 00:50:41.070
aimed at improving the quality of people.

00:50:41.070 --> 00:50:44.450
This combination has made the
difference in Latin America.

00:50:44.450 --> 00:50:47.160
With economic growth, governments
now have had resources

00:50:47.160 --> 00:50:52.160
to invest in infrastructure development,

00:50:53.100 --> 00:50:56.450
which means better schools,
better hospitals, better roads.

00:50:56.450 --> 00:50:59.120
This transformation has
been because of that.

00:50:59.120 --> 00:51:04.090
Now looking forward, once again,
the region is not isolated.

00:51:04.090 --> 00:51:07.950
It is part of what is taking
place on a global scale.

00:51:07.950 --> 00:51:09.950
Much of the success in the last decade

00:51:09.950 --> 00:51:13.220
has been that southern countries,

00:51:13.220 --> 00:51:16.990
Brazil, Chile, Peru, Argentina,

00:51:16.990 --> 00:51:20.780
have had close trade links with China.

00:51:20.780 --> 00:51:22.990
China has made an an inroad

00:51:22.990 --> 00:51:24.710
within Latin America and the Caribbean.

00:51:24.710 --> 00:51:27.350
And I will say South
America at this moment

00:51:27.350 --> 00:51:32.200
is more connected with China
and Asia than ever before.

00:51:32.200 --> 00:51:34.750
Now Mexico, Central
America, and the Caribbean

00:51:34.750 --> 00:51:38.554
are more integrated with the US economy.

00:51:38.554 --> 00:51:41.947
The Dominican Republic is
more integrated with the US,

00:51:41.947 --> 00:51:43.950
and of course that has
to do with geography.

00:51:43.950 --> 00:51:47.310
It's the geographical proximity
that we have with the US.

00:51:47.310 --> 00:51:51.440
It also has to do with the
presence of a population here.

00:51:51.440 --> 00:51:53.150
Central America, the Caribbean islands

00:51:53.150 --> 00:51:58.110
have a Latino diaspora
that connects with the US

00:51:58.110 --> 00:52:00.700
and their respective countries
is less South America,

00:52:00.700 --> 00:52:03.490
which always had more close relationship

00:52:03.490 --> 00:52:05.740
with the European
countries than with the US.

00:52:06.730 --> 00:52:09.350
So if there is a deceleration
of economic growth,

00:52:09.350 --> 00:52:11.150
in China, for example, or in Asia,

00:52:11.150 --> 00:52:13.150
these countries will be hurt

00:52:13.150 --> 00:52:15.940
because their exports
have been going to China.

00:52:15.940 --> 00:52:17.608
If China demands less copper

00:52:17.608 --> 00:52:20.790
and less zinc and less foodstuff,

00:52:20.790 --> 00:52:22.630
so their economies will drop.

00:52:22.630 --> 00:52:24.760
And we already can see that.

00:52:24.760 --> 00:52:27.130
China was growing 12% a year.

00:52:27.130 --> 00:52:30.250
This past year decelerated to 7%,

00:52:30.250 --> 00:52:34.490
and now the Brazilian economy
has only grown 1.5% in 2012.

00:52:34.490 --> 00:52:36.343
So we depend on that.

00:52:37.330 --> 00:52:39.450
The future of Latin
America then first of all

00:52:39.450 --> 00:52:42.720
will depend on how the
global economy performs.

00:52:42.720 --> 00:52:44.780
If the US economy picks up again,

00:52:44.780 --> 00:52:46.960
and you can have 2.53% growth,

00:52:46.960 --> 00:52:49.110
that will have a tremendous
impact in Latin America,

00:52:49.110 --> 00:52:50.720
especially in Mexico.

00:52:50.720 --> 00:52:53.360
If the US economy starts booming up again,

00:52:53.360 --> 00:52:57.066
it will pick up on Mexico
that is poised to be

00:52:57.066 --> 00:53:00.070
also a country with a great potential

00:53:00.070 --> 00:53:03.470
for economic growth and
sustainability in the years to come.

00:53:03.470 --> 00:53:06.240
If Europe can get out of
its current recession,

00:53:06.240 --> 00:53:07.600
it will also have an impact

00:53:07.600 --> 00:53:08.760
in Latin America and the Caribbean.

00:53:08.760 --> 00:53:10.810
Now on the domestic side,

00:53:10.810 --> 00:53:12.930
we have had a decade of great success,

00:53:12.930 --> 00:53:14.390
but also we have some challenges.

00:53:14.390 --> 00:53:16.183
The question of sustainability.

00:53:17.110 --> 00:53:19.800
What is, the question of sustainability

00:53:19.800 --> 00:53:21.330
for Latin American and Caribbean economy

00:53:21.330 --> 00:53:23.370
has to do with the fact that

00:53:23.370 --> 00:53:26.430
we have been exporting raw materials.

00:53:26.430 --> 00:53:28.460
We have been exporting natural resources

00:53:28.460 --> 00:53:30.720
with no productive transformation.

00:53:30.720 --> 00:53:32.950
So the real thing now is

00:53:32.950 --> 00:53:36.080
how can we add value to
our products and services?

00:53:36.080 --> 00:53:39.480
How can we be part of a
global chain of production?

00:53:39.480 --> 00:53:41.890
And that can only be
done through knowledge.

00:53:41.890 --> 00:53:44.360
So the quality of education is key.

00:53:44.360 --> 00:53:46.890
Improving our universities.

00:53:46.890 --> 00:53:48.840
We are very preoccupied with,

00:53:48.840 --> 00:53:50.550
with the standard of our universities

00:53:50.550 --> 00:53:51.620
in Latin America in the Caribbean.

00:53:51.620 --> 00:53:55.470
When you see university rankings,
world university rankings,

00:53:55.470 --> 00:53:57.250
the first one that appears in the ranking

00:53:57.250 --> 00:53:58.755
from Latin America and the Caribbean is

00:53:58.755 --> 00:54:02.280
(speaks foreign language)

00:54:02.280 --> 00:54:06.150
and it figures out number 200, number 200.

00:54:06.150 --> 00:54:08.924
And then after that Sao Paulo is there,

00:54:08.924 --> 00:54:11.380
(speaks foreign language)

00:54:11.380 --> 00:54:13.320
but all after the 200.

00:54:13.320 --> 00:54:16.200
So we're not competing internationally

00:54:16.200 --> 00:54:18.350
in terms of producing knowledge

00:54:18.350 --> 00:54:23.010
and generating new
patents to be registered,

00:54:23.010 --> 00:54:25.830
intellectual property
rights, we're lacking there.

00:54:25.830 --> 00:54:27.610
So Latin America needs a policy

00:54:27.610 --> 00:54:30.723
for science, technology, and innovation.

00:54:31.720 --> 00:54:33.750
We need to protect our
indigenous knowledge

00:54:33.750 --> 00:54:36.440
with new intellectual property rights.

00:54:36.440 --> 00:54:37.740
This is the way to move forward,

00:54:37.740 --> 00:54:40.470
and for that we need
public/private partnerships.

00:54:40.470 --> 00:54:43.850
In order to invest in innovation
and technology and science

00:54:43.850 --> 00:54:46.050
we need to create a culture of science.

00:54:46.050 --> 00:54:47.540
In Latin America over the years

00:54:47.540 --> 00:54:49.130
we have been promoting soft science.

00:54:49.130 --> 00:54:51.910
Everybody studies law or studies marketing

00:54:51.910 --> 00:54:54.730
or studies management, soft science.

00:54:54.730 --> 00:54:58.230
Now we need engineers, we need scientists

00:54:58.230 --> 00:54:59.920
that can put all their knowledge in place

00:54:59.920 --> 00:55:03.780
and really make this
transformative production

00:55:03.780 --> 00:55:06.010
of our raw materials into
new goods and services.

00:55:06.010 --> 00:55:08.640
If we are able to improve
the quality of education,

00:55:08.640 --> 00:55:11.450
to innovate, to integrate
within the global economy,

00:55:11.450 --> 00:55:13.540
then I think Latin America will become

00:55:13.540 --> 00:55:15.623
a major key player in global affairs.

00:55:22.150 --> 00:55:23.160
- [Man] You ended your speech

00:55:23.160 --> 00:55:25.310
speaking about Latinos in America.

00:55:25.310 --> 00:55:28.723
Which is a growing population, huge.

00:55:29.899 --> 00:55:32.410
And you talked a little
bit about the 16.7%

00:55:32.410 --> 00:55:34.453
of the population is Latino here.

00:55:35.360 --> 00:55:38.969
Fortune 500 company CEOs is only 1.7%.

00:55:38.969 --> 00:55:42.500
That's six CEOs for the Fortune 500.

00:55:42.500 --> 00:55:45.230
What steps outside of
education and mentorship

00:55:45.230 --> 00:55:47.537
because there's very few mentors

00:55:47.537 --> 00:55:49.300
because not a lot of people
that are in that position.

00:55:49.300 --> 00:55:53.133
But what other steps can
we take to bridge that gap?

00:55:54.320 --> 00:55:57.480
- I think that is the main
road ahead is education.

00:55:57.480 --> 00:55:58.863
It has to do with,

00:55:59.870 --> 00:56:03.440
with innovation, with vision forward,

00:56:03.440 --> 00:56:05.310
and creating some sort of

00:56:05.310 --> 00:56:07.950
Latino organizations that already exists,

00:56:07.950 --> 00:56:11.070
and supporting each other and
coming out with new ventures,

00:56:11.070 --> 00:56:12.970
coming out with new fresh ideas

00:56:12.970 --> 00:56:14.740
that will have an impact in the US.

00:56:14.740 --> 00:56:17.940
So but the key to everything
has to do with education.

00:56:17.940 --> 00:56:19.110
And perhaps we're not there

00:56:19.110 --> 00:56:22.250
as CEOs in those Fortune 500 companies

00:56:22.250 --> 00:56:24.190
because there is still not some gap

00:56:24.190 --> 00:56:26.420
in terms of knowledge
between the Latino community

00:56:26.420 --> 00:56:28.700
and other communities here in the US.

00:56:28.700 --> 00:56:31.380
I will always emphasize
the role of education

00:56:31.380 --> 00:56:33.190
to move up the ladder.

00:56:33.190 --> 00:56:34.400
You can see that, for example,

00:56:34.400 --> 00:56:38.770
you have Sonia Sotomayor coming
out a graduate from Yale,

00:56:38.770 --> 00:56:41.800
but coming from Yale, she
makes it to the Supreme Court.

00:56:41.800 --> 00:56:42.633
You see?

00:56:43.920 --> 00:56:45.220
So it's education at the end.

00:56:45.220 --> 00:56:47.270
At the bottom of everything is education.

00:56:51.522 --> 00:56:53.759
- [Man] Mr. President,

00:56:53.759 --> 00:56:55.070
Good afternoon, Mr. President.

00:56:55.070 --> 00:56:57.490
Talking about the future of Latin America,

00:56:57.490 --> 00:57:00.600
how do you foresee the strengthening

00:57:00.600 --> 00:57:04.270
of regional groups to participate

00:57:04.270 --> 00:57:06.840
as a block in the global economy?

00:57:06.840 --> 00:57:10.750
The GDP of El Salvador is
not the same as Argentina,

00:57:10.750 --> 00:57:14.310
or the GDP of Jamaica is
not the same as Brazil.

00:57:14.310 --> 00:57:19.310
And those, our economies are growing

00:57:19.570 --> 00:57:23.610
because of a demand of
resources from China,

00:57:23.610 --> 00:57:26.913
the resources that we held in our lands.

00:57:27.930 --> 00:57:31.690
Arable land, potable
water, precious metals,

00:57:31.690 --> 00:57:35.660
non-precious metals, are going
to be demanded in the future

00:57:35.660 --> 00:57:37.570
from Europe, from the United States,

00:57:37.570 --> 00:57:39.200
and other parts of the world.

00:57:39.200 --> 00:57:42.040
So wouldn't the Latin America benefit

00:57:42.040 --> 00:57:47.040
from a cohesive union,
a group such as UNASUR,

00:57:47.205 --> 00:57:49.780
but at much larger scale?

00:57:49.780 --> 00:57:51.670
Don't you see, when you foresee that

00:57:51.670 --> 00:57:56.060
regional grouping Latin American countries

00:57:56.060 --> 00:57:58.720
will benefit the region as a whole?

00:57:58.720 --> 00:57:59.822
Thank you.

00:57:59.822 --> 00:58:03.450
- Yeah, I totally agree there
is an integration process

00:58:03.450 --> 00:58:05.790
taking place in Latin
America at different levels.

00:58:05.790 --> 00:58:09.063
It has always existed
at sub-regional levels.

00:58:09.900 --> 00:58:13.460
The Caribbean countries, for
example, are within CARICOM.

00:58:13.460 --> 00:58:16.040
If you add Dominican
Republic, Haiti, and Cuba,

00:58:16.040 --> 00:58:17.670
it's called CARIFORUM,

00:58:17.670 --> 00:58:19.130
and they use it as a platform

00:58:19.130 --> 00:58:21.630
to negotiate with the European Union.

00:58:21.630 --> 00:58:24.077
Then you have in Central America SICA,

00:58:24.077 --> 00:58:27.660
(speaks foreign language)
is also integration.

00:58:27.660 --> 00:58:31.030
If you add Mexico to Central America,

00:58:31.030 --> 00:58:34.530
it's called Mesoamerica,
and they also negotiate.

00:58:34.530 --> 00:58:38.099
And in South America you
have the Andean Pact,

00:58:38.099 --> 00:58:39.699
(speaks foreign language)

00:58:39.699 --> 00:58:41.910
and then you have Mercosur in the south,

00:58:41.910 --> 00:58:43.900
and now they have created UNASUR.

00:58:43.900 --> 00:58:46.920
The problem is that we
have too many organizations

00:58:46.920 --> 00:58:49.500
that are overlapping
each one with the other.

00:58:49.500 --> 00:58:51.470
Politically we have created something new

00:58:51.470 --> 00:58:53.650
that I hope it can work, it's called CELAC

00:58:54.619 --> 00:58:57.345
(speaks foreign language)

00:58:57.345 --> 00:59:00.000
And CELAC is supposed to
be one voice representing

00:59:00.000 --> 00:59:01.910
politically political dialogue

00:59:01.910 --> 00:59:04.140
Latin America with the rest of the world.

00:59:04.140 --> 00:59:08.400
What's lacking is that it's
not a formalized institution.

00:59:08.400 --> 00:59:11.530
The chair changes every year
from one country to the other.

00:59:11.530 --> 00:59:14.760
So I think perhaps we
have to work more on that

00:59:14.760 --> 00:59:17.190
to have a one voice
representative of Latin America.

00:59:17.190 --> 00:59:18.520
I'll give you an example.

00:59:18.520 --> 00:59:21.100
We have three countries
as I mentioned before,

00:59:21.100 --> 00:59:23.710
Brazil, Mexico, and Argentina,

00:59:23.710 --> 00:59:26.040
that are members of the G20 group.

00:59:26.040 --> 00:59:27.860
How proud are we as Latin Americans

00:59:27.860 --> 00:59:31.650
to have three countries
there in that elite club?

00:59:31.650 --> 00:59:34.270
Nevertheless when they
go to those meetings,

00:59:34.270 --> 00:59:35.680
they go with their own agenda,

00:59:35.680 --> 00:59:37.500
they don't represent Latin
America and the Caribbean.

00:59:37.500 --> 00:59:41.610
We hope that within
CELAC we can get together

00:59:41.610 --> 00:59:44.960
and design a policy that will represent

00:59:44.960 --> 00:59:46.210
the interests of all Latin America.

00:59:46.210 --> 00:59:48.250
That doesn't exist until now.

00:59:48.250 --> 00:59:52.710
So there is, I see the
idea of integrating,

00:59:52.710 --> 00:59:56.070
the idea of cooperation,
south-south cooperation,

00:59:56.070 --> 00:59:57.600
creating new institutions,

00:59:57.600 --> 01:00:00.160
for example financial institutions

01:00:00.160 --> 01:00:03.400
that will finance
projects in Latin America

01:00:03.400 --> 01:00:07.260
with I would say conditions that are not

01:00:07.260 --> 01:00:09.060
within the commercial banks.

01:00:09.060 --> 01:00:10.870
These kinds of things are interesting

01:00:10.870 --> 01:00:13.683
also in the energy sector for example.

01:00:14.870 --> 01:00:17.340
You can have it in educational exchange

01:00:17.340 --> 01:00:19.270
within Latin American countries.

01:00:19.270 --> 01:00:20.780
So these are all new initiatives

01:00:20.780 --> 01:00:22.440
that we did not have before.

01:00:22.440 --> 01:00:24.780
The idea of cooperation,
the idea of solidarity,

01:00:24.780 --> 01:00:26.350
the idea of coming together,

01:00:26.350 --> 01:00:28.690
is something also new in the region

01:00:28.690 --> 01:00:31.120
that is enhancing our possibilities

01:00:31.120 --> 01:00:34.133
of increasing our economic
growth and social well-being.

01:00:40.074 --> 01:00:41.730
- [Woman] (speaks foreign
language) It is a real honor

01:00:41.730 --> 01:00:44.030
to have you here at Yale,
so thank you for being here,

01:00:44.030 --> 01:00:46.170
and thanks for all the Dominicans

01:00:46.170 --> 01:00:48.360
who are here visiting as well.

01:00:48.360 --> 01:00:50.350
I would like to invite you to discuss

01:00:50.350 --> 01:00:52.170
a little bit more the conflict you noted

01:00:52.170 --> 01:00:55.510
between neoliberalism and neopopulism.

01:00:55.510 --> 01:00:58.090
I'm particularly
interested in how you view

01:00:58.090 --> 01:01:01.300
this translating into the
global trade dynamics,

01:01:01.300 --> 01:01:03.970
and what types of trade agreements

01:01:03.970 --> 01:01:05.470
Latin American and Caribbean countries,

01:01:05.470 --> 01:01:07.670
and specifically Dominican Republic,

01:01:07.670 --> 01:01:09.150
if you wish to comment on that,

01:01:09.150 --> 01:01:11.570
would view as advantageous.

01:01:11.570 --> 01:01:14.530
For example, one might
associate neoliberalism

01:01:14.530 --> 01:01:18.500
with the incredible rise in
bilateral investment treaties

01:01:18.500 --> 01:01:21.260
that was on the 90s and early 2000,

01:01:22.550 --> 01:01:24.260
the multilateral trade agreement

01:01:24.260 --> 01:01:28.190
such as DR-CAFTA and NAFTA,
and now in more recent years

01:01:28.190 --> 01:01:33.060
with one might say the
neopopulism rise in Latin America,

01:01:33.060 --> 01:01:36.820
we see at least two if not
three Latin American countries

01:01:36.820 --> 01:01:39.440
rejecting and challenging ICSID,

01:01:39.440 --> 01:01:42.270
the world bank dispute
resolution framework

01:01:42.270 --> 01:01:46.033
for investment state treaty disputes.

01:01:48.864 --> 01:01:50.630
And we see South Africa,

01:01:50.630 --> 01:01:55.630
which obviously not part of
the region but a notable actor

01:01:55.660 --> 01:01:58.370
who as I understand it
has called for an end

01:01:58.370 --> 01:02:01.520
to entering into bilateral
investment treaties.

01:02:01.520 --> 01:02:04.393
So how does this all translate into,

01:02:05.640 --> 01:02:10.640
what types of agreements
are Latin American countries

01:02:10.960 --> 01:02:12.760
and Caribbean countries in particular

01:02:12.760 --> 01:02:16.470
rethinking as advantageous
to its own interest,

01:02:16.470 --> 01:02:19.519
and how do you equate that with this

01:02:19.519 --> 01:02:22.743
neopopulism neoliberalism
divide that you've noted?

01:02:23.830 --> 01:02:27.850
- I think there's a combination
of different elements here.

01:02:27.850 --> 01:02:31.220
It has to do with political ideology,

01:02:31.220 --> 01:02:33.490
it has to do with economic interests,

01:02:33.490 --> 01:02:36.940
and also with a particular
moment in history,

01:02:36.940 --> 01:02:39.150
in current contemporary history.

01:02:39.150 --> 01:02:41.793
For example, looking from the trade side,

01:02:42.730 --> 01:02:43.927
we had in Latin America

01:02:43.927 --> 01:02:45.860
and the Caribbean, more specifically,

01:02:45.860 --> 01:02:49.223
what was called the Caribbean
Basin Initiative or CBI.

01:02:50.410 --> 01:02:53.490
With CBI, at all the Caribbean
countries were allowed

01:02:53.490 --> 01:02:56.270
to export their goods and services

01:02:56.270 --> 01:03:00.780
into the US market on a
duty-free basis unilaterally,

01:03:00.780 --> 01:03:03.020
with the exception of Nicaragua and Cuba,

01:03:03.020 --> 01:03:06.123
excluded for ideological
political reasons at the time.

01:03:07.060 --> 01:03:11.730
But the thing is that
there was an incentive

01:03:11.730 --> 01:03:14.510
to export within the
largest market in the world,

01:03:14.510 --> 01:03:17.770
which is the US market, unilaterally.

01:03:17.770 --> 01:03:20.940
This was done to help
the Caribbean countries

01:03:20.940 --> 01:03:23.650
overcome their internal problems,

01:03:23.650 --> 01:03:26.770
and somehow to stop
massive illegal migration

01:03:26.770 --> 01:03:29.420
coming from the Caribbean into the US.

01:03:29.420 --> 01:03:31.480
This was the logic of all this.

01:03:31.480 --> 01:03:34.610
Now after the end of the Cold War,

01:03:34.610 --> 01:03:36.930
a unilateral free trade access

01:03:36.930 --> 01:03:39.320
into the US market
coming from the Caribbean

01:03:39.320 --> 01:03:43.360
becomes a reciprocal free trade treaty

01:03:43.360 --> 01:03:46.590
between the Caribbean
and the United States.

01:03:46.590 --> 01:03:50.280
And this creates a
problem in the sense that

01:03:50.280 --> 01:03:53.950
we're dealing with an economy of scale,

01:03:53.950 --> 01:03:55.950
which would be the US economy,

01:03:55.950 --> 01:04:00.950
with very small, poverty-ridden
economies in the Caribbean.

01:04:02.594 --> 01:04:04.780
And this sense of a symmetry or disparity,

01:04:04.780 --> 01:04:07.080
how can it be tackled?

01:04:07.080 --> 01:04:08.810
And of course there is no answer to that,

01:04:08.810 --> 01:04:10.690
and I'll just give you an example.

01:04:10.690 --> 01:04:13.690
I think I talked to you
with our students yesterday

01:04:13.690 --> 01:04:16.053
about Haiti, for you to understand.

01:04:17.160 --> 01:04:21.033
Haitian workers produce rice
in the Dominican Republic.

01:04:21.970 --> 01:04:25.260
But they don't do it in Haiti, why not?

01:04:25.260 --> 01:04:29.280
Because Haiti imports all
its rice from Arkansas,

01:04:29.280 --> 01:04:33.520
which is subsidized by
the local government,

01:04:33.520 --> 01:04:35.610
the production and the export.

01:04:35.610 --> 01:04:38.950
So it's cheaper for Haiti to import rice,

01:04:38.950 --> 01:04:40.510
subsidized rice from Arkansas

01:04:40.510 --> 01:04:42.700
than to produce that themselves.

01:04:42.700 --> 01:04:45.400
So in that case, it is
very difficult to have

01:04:45.400 --> 01:04:48.810
really real free trade
when there is a disparity

01:04:48.810 --> 01:04:52.620
or asymmetry between two
different scale of economies.

01:04:52.620 --> 01:04:54.450
This is a problem for the future.

01:04:54.450 --> 01:04:56.970
Because even though it has
been considered gradually

01:04:56.970 --> 01:04:59.930
that the economy will open gradually

01:04:59.930 --> 01:05:03.070
to the import of us goods and services,

01:05:03.070 --> 01:05:05.490
also Europe because we have signed

01:05:05.490 --> 01:05:07.740
free trade agreements with Europe,

01:05:07.740 --> 01:05:10.020
it will take 20, 25 years they say

01:05:10.020 --> 01:05:12.170
to have fully opened the markets

01:05:12.170 --> 01:05:13.720
to the imports of goods and services.

01:05:13.720 --> 01:05:17.050
But even then, in 25 years from now,

01:05:17.050 --> 01:05:20.870
many of our productive
sectors will be harmed

01:05:20.870 --> 01:05:22.310
by the opening up of this market.

01:05:22.310 --> 01:05:23.950
So this is something that's still pending

01:05:23.950 --> 01:05:26.550
that we have to work on and see exactly

01:05:26.550 --> 01:05:31.550
how we can benefit our
population back in the Caribbean.

01:05:31.560 --> 01:05:34.280
Finally on neoliberalism and neopopulism.

01:05:34.280 --> 01:05:35.960
I think at the center stage

01:05:35.960 --> 01:05:38.900
of political philosophical
debate worldwide

01:05:38.900 --> 01:05:41.233
is the relationship
between state and market.

01:05:42.450 --> 01:05:44.550
It is a relationship between

01:05:44.550 --> 01:05:47.140
a conservative movement which thinks that

01:05:47.140 --> 01:05:49.740
markets should by themselves

01:05:49.740 --> 01:05:51.810
determine the outcome and performance

01:05:51.810 --> 01:05:53.800
of societies in every aspect,

01:05:53.800 --> 01:05:57.793
that markets are self-regulating,
even though what happened

01:05:57.793 --> 01:05:59.670
with the recent global financial crisis

01:05:59.670 --> 01:06:03.130
proves that markets are
not self-regulating.

01:06:03.130 --> 01:06:05.560
And those that have gone
to the other extreme

01:06:05.560 --> 01:06:09.860
to think that the states
should play the pivotal role

01:06:09.860 --> 01:06:12.420
in any decision-making that is required

01:06:12.420 --> 01:06:15.630
by the economic sector of by society.

01:06:15.630 --> 01:06:20.569
We call those that have
made the faith in the market

01:06:20.569 --> 01:06:23.890
as the only solution to every problem

01:06:23.890 --> 01:06:25.543
the neoliberal proposal,

01:06:26.430 --> 01:06:28.790
and the other which has a state-centric

01:06:28.790 --> 01:06:31.840
solution to everything is a neopopulist.

01:06:31.840 --> 01:06:34.490
The idea is that both are needed,

01:06:34.490 --> 01:06:36.570
you need a market to operate,

01:06:36.570 --> 01:06:40.630
but you also need a strong
government to intervene

01:06:40.630 --> 01:06:44.130
on occasional, on specific occasions

01:06:44.130 --> 01:06:47.160
in order to guarantee
some sort of equilibrium,

01:06:47.160 --> 01:06:48.920
social equilibrium within society

01:06:48.920 --> 01:06:51.600
that you do through your tax system.

01:06:51.600 --> 01:06:53.700
So the combination of state and market

01:06:53.700 --> 01:06:55.750
will give us a new paradigm

01:06:55.750 --> 01:06:58.750
going beyond neoliberalism
and neopopulism.

01:06:58.750 --> 01:07:01.000
There are positive things in both.

01:07:01.000 --> 01:07:02.980
I mean you cannot condemn and reject

01:07:02.980 --> 01:07:03.990
everything that comes from

01:07:03.990 --> 01:07:06.050
a liberal view or conservative view.

01:07:06.050 --> 01:07:07.580
Clinton here in the US

01:07:07.580 --> 01:07:10.280
took some ideas from the Reagan movement.

01:07:10.280 --> 01:07:13.440
Tony Blair in the UK took some
ideas from Margaret Thatcher.

01:07:13.440 --> 01:07:14.910
He came into the center.

01:07:14.910 --> 01:07:17.700
If you are able to
balance the relationship

01:07:17.700 --> 01:07:21.160
between state and market, you
might become more effective

01:07:21.160 --> 01:07:23.560
and really satisfy people's needs

01:07:23.560 --> 01:07:26.630
in terms of opportunities
and growth and etc.

01:07:26.630 --> 01:07:27.840
So this is what I think.

01:07:27.840 --> 01:07:30.220
It's not only Latin
America, it's worldwide,

01:07:30.220 --> 01:07:33.043
this debate about the role
of the state in the market.

01:07:33.880 --> 01:07:36.340
- [Jeff] I think we're gonna
have time for one more question

01:07:36.340 --> 01:07:38.257
so let's make it a really terrific one.

01:07:38.257 --> 01:07:40.206
(laughing)

01:07:40.206 --> 01:07:41.625
- I think there's a question over here.

01:07:41.625 --> 01:07:43.458
- [Jeff] Be confident.

01:07:45.025 --> 01:07:45.934
- There's a question over here.

01:07:45.934 --> 01:07:46.767
- [Man] Mr. President?

01:07:46.767 --> 01:07:47.600
- Yes?

01:07:47.600 --> 01:07:48.433
- [Man] I have a two-part question.

01:07:48.433 --> 01:07:50.700
The first is I'm wondering
whether you can comment

01:07:50.700 --> 01:07:53.870
on the drug problem in Latin America,

01:07:53.870 --> 01:07:55.920
and any solutions you might (mumbling).

01:07:57.630 --> 01:08:01.311
The second part is, do
you like Bachata music?

01:08:01.311 --> 01:08:03.220
(audience laughing)

01:08:03.220 --> 01:08:04.450
- Alright.

01:08:04.450 --> 01:08:06.620
I'll respond the last one which is easier.

01:08:06.620 --> 01:08:07.613
Of course I do.

01:08:08.880 --> 01:08:10.470
Bachata.

01:08:10.470 --> 01:08:12.560
On the first one, I
think is one of the most

01:08:12.560 --> 01:08:13.740
challenging problems were having

01:08:13.740 --> 01:08:16.270
in Latin America and
the Caribbean right now,

01:08:16.270 --> 01:08:17.840
the drug trafficking issue,

01:08:17.840 --> 01:08:21.260
and violence related to drug trafficking.

01:08:21.260 --> 01:08:23.700
And the only way I think
this can be addressed

01:08:23.700 --> 01:08:25.080
is through a partnership

01:08:25.080 --> 01:08:27.463
between the US and our
countries back home.

01:08:29.610 --> 01:08:33.880
There has been at some
points a marked interest

01:08:33.880 --> 01:08:36.230
in building up in this partnership.

01:08:36.230 --> 01:08:38.923
But nevertheless, I think
much more can be done.

01:08:39.980 --> 01:08:43.360
Over more than 20 years
of addressing this issue

01:08:43.360 --> 01:08:46.080
with a certain perspective,
it has not worked out.

01:08:46.080 --> 01:08:50.020
I think a new set of fresh
ideas must be put in place.

01:08:50.020 --> 01:08:52.960
There is a need to enhance the dialogue

01:08:52.960 --> 01:08:54.347
between Latin America and the US

01:08:54.347 --> 01:08:56.300
on how to tackle this problem.

01:08:56.300 --> 01:08:57.590
Now yesterday in the press

01:08:57.590 --> 01:09:00.220
I read something that really concerned me

01:09:01.270 --> 01:09:02.910
that is taking place here in the US.

01:09:02.910 --> 01:09:06.230
Because from Latin America
we have the supply side,

01:09:06.230 --> 01:09:09.480
in the US we have the
demand side of this market.

01:09:09.480 --> 01:09:11.780
Yesterday in USA Today,

01:09:11.780 --> 01:09:15.310
I read that a group of young businessmen

01:09:15.310 --> 01:09:20.310
were creating a corporation
equity investment

01:09:21.690 --> 01:09:24.160
to buy off some companies

01:09:24.160 --> 01:09:27.240
and from there purchase
marijuana in the US,

01:09:27.240 --> 01:09:30.140
which will be legalized in many states.

01:09:30.140 --> 01:09:32.650
So when you think that
there is a group already

01:09:32.650 --> 01:09:35.000
of people thinking here and doing business

01:09:35.000 --> 01:09:37.770
and making profit out
of selling marijuana,

01:09:37.770 --> 01:09:39.500
it might indicate that there is a trend

01:09:39.500 --> 01:09:42.466
to the legalization of drugs

01:09:42.466 --> 01:09:46.110
for business purposes and profit making,

01:09:46.110 --> 01:09:49.270
which I think would be
a wrong thing to do.

01:09:49.270 --> 01:09:52.510
I think drugs is not alcohol.

01:09:52.510 --> 01:09:54.680
We all know that drugs has an impact

01:09:54.680 --> 01:09:57.730
on people's brains and people's behavior.

01:09:57.730 --> 01:10:00.293
It also has a health
issue, healthcare issue.

01:10:00.293 --> 01:10:02.610
It's not only the business side

01:10:02.610 --> 01:10:04.450
or the criminal side of it.

01:10:04.450 --> 01:10:06.980
It has to do with people's
behavior, people's attitudes,

01:10:06.980 --> 01:10:11.980
people's way of relating
to each other in society.

01:10:12.140 --> 01:10:15.030
So it's still a problem.

01:10:15.030 --> 01:10:17.050
It has not been addressed effectively.

01:10:17.050 --> 01:10:18.420
When you look at the way

01:10:18.420 --> 01:10:21.327
the tourist issue is being dealt with,

01:10:21.327 --> 01:10:25.270
and you look at the way the
drug lords are being treated

01:10:25.270 --> 01:10:26.890
there is a difference.

01:10:26.890 --> 01:10:30.520
I think we have to look at the drug lords

01:10:30.520 --> 01:10:31.970
and the tourists the same way

01:10:31.970 --> 01:10:35.580
because they represent both
a threat to the well-being

01:10:35.580 --> 01:10:37.000
and stability of our societies,

01:10:37.000 --> 01:10:38.170
and I don't think that

01:10:38.170 --> 01:10:40.420
they are looking at the
problem the same way.

01:10:42.870 --> 01:10:45.950
- [Woman] Okay, so Mr. President,

01:10:45.950 --> 01:10:49.220
so a lot of your focus is on education.

01:10:49.220 --> 01:10:52.430
And relating to a question
that was asked earlier,

01:10:52.430 --> 01:10:55.850
about the Fortune 500 and the
representation of Latinos.

01:10:55.850 --> 01:10:57.050
Latinos aren't represented

01:10:57.050 --> 01:11:00.180
in a majority of the
high-profile positions,

01:11:00.180 --> 01:11:02.640
and as you said, this
relates to education.

01:11:02.640 --> 01:11:04.310
Latinos here in the United States

01:11:04.310 --> 01:11:08.710
have the highest dropout rate
of any ethnic or racial group.

01:11:08.710 --> 01:11:11.230
And so a lot of that,

01:11:11.230 --> 01:11:12.630
there are many factors going into that.

01:11:12.630 --> 01:11:14.980
Economics, social class.

01:11:14.980 --> 01:11:16.390
But part of it does have to do with

01:11:16.390 --> 01:11:20.550
the discrepancy between education
here in the United States

01:11:20.550 --> 01:11:22.960
and in the home countries.

01:11:22.960 --> 01:11:26.240
So since the US population of Latinos

01:11:26.240 --> 01:11:28.830
is going to increase
so much in the future,

01:11:28.830 --> 01:11:30.080
this is obviously of importance

01:11:30.080 --> 01:11:32.250
to both the US and to Latin America.

01:11:32.250 --> 01:11:34.140
So in what ways do you think

01:11:34.140 --> 01:11:36.693
Latin America can
improve on its education?

01:11:42.040 --> 01:11:43.383
- What you see now,

01:11:45.090 --> 01:11:48.030
Latinos within the US,

01:11:48.030 --> 01:11:50.210
making progress in some sense, no?

01:11:50.210 --> 01:11:52.370
Even though still when you
look at the statistics,

01:11:52.370 --> 01:11:54.990
you're below some other groups.

01:11:54.990 --> 01:11:59.330
It has to do with the way

01:11:59.330 --> 01:12:02.320
a wave of migration
came into this country.

01:12:02.320 --> 01:12:06.090
The group that has
traditionally migrated are those

01:12:06.090 --> 01:12:09.850
that are underclass in Latin
America and the Caribbean.

01:12:09.850 --> 01:12:13.550
Those are the poor who
have migrated into the US.

01:12:13.550 --> 01:12:17.140
So it has taken them a
while to get integrated

01:12:17.140 --> 01:12:21.800
within a modern society as the US society.

01:12:21.800 --> 01:12:23.340
You have to learn a new language,

01:12:23.340 --> 01:12:24.620
you have to learn a new culture,

01:12:24.620 --> 01:12:26.410
you have to be part of the system,

01:12:26.410 --> 01:12:28.550
in order to really participate.

01:12:28.550 --> 01:12:31.180
So it has taken I would say, 30, 40 years

01:12:32.170 --> 01:12:35.740
for our people to be part
of the American Dream,

01:12:35.740 --> 01:12:38.030
to be part of the American system.

01:12:38.030 --> 01:12:42.740
Now a second and third
generation has born in the US.

01:12:42.740 --> 01:12:44.550
I think it's your case.

01:12:44.550 --> 01:12:45.470
And look what is happening.

01:12:45.470 --> 01:12:48.163
Now we have Dominicans at Yale University.

01:12:49.110 --> 01:12:52.120
It is, you would have
never thought about having

01:12:52.120 --> 01:12:57.120
Latinos or Dominicans at Yale
University 20, 25 years ago.

01:12:57.270 --> 01:12:58.670
It was impossible.

01:12:58.670 --> 01:12:59.693
Now you're here,

01:13:02.550 --> 01:13:05.170
Master Brenzel, and if the
years, in the years to come,

01:13:05.170 --> 01:13:06.660
these Dominicans most likely will be

01:13:06.660 --> 01:13:10.770
deans and masters here at Yale University

01:13:10.770 --> 01:13:15.370
because of your, I would
say, your innovative spirit,

01:13:15.370 --> 01:13:17.053
and your capacity to work.

01:13:18.150 --> 01:13:22.110
So looking into the future,
we'll have a new generation

01:13:22.110 --> 01:13:25.030
of American-born Latinos,

01:13:25.030 --> 01:13:28.750
which will have a great command
of the English language,

01:13:28.750 --> 01:13:31.107
will be bi-cultural from the beginning,

01:13:31.107 --> 01:13:33.580
and will have more
opportunities and access

01:13:33.580 --> 01:13:36.110
in this society to move up the ladder.

01:13:36.110 --> 01:13:38.200
So the future of Latinos in the US

01:13:38.200 --> 01:13:41.750
is much brighter than
what has been up until now

01:13:41.750 --> 01:13:44.070
because new opportunities
will come to them.

01:13:44.070 --> 01:13:45.660
It is already happening.

01:13:45.660 --> 01:13:47.530
I mean, just think of the idea that

01:13:47.530 --> 01:13:51.650
we have a Latino justice
of the Supreme Court.

01:13:51.650 --> 01:13:54.500
Just think of the idea
that when President Obama

01:13:54.500 --> 01:13:58.110
addressed the State of the Union
Address, who responded him?

01:13:58.110 --> 01:14:00.760
A Latino from the Republican
party, Marco Rubio,

01:14:00.760 --> 01:14:02.780
the senator from Florida.

01:14:02.780 --> 01:14:06.410
And now the Republicans
are thinking to put

01:14:06.410 --> 01:14:09.940
a Latino on the electoral ticket in 2016

01:14:09.940 --> 01:14:12.680
to compete with the Democratic party.

01:14:12.680 --> 01:14:14.950
So the Latinos are really moving up there.

01:14:14.950 --> 01:14:18.703
They're being considered
an important sector in US.

01:14:20.420 --> 01:14:23.250
But I insist, in order
to really make a mark

01:14:23.250 --> 01:14:26.630
and really to move on as a community,

01:14:26.630 --> 01:14:29.390
as families, as
individuals, as communities,

01:14:29.390 --> 01:14:31.830
the key to success is education.

01:14:31.830 --> 01:14:34.890
Back in our countries
also the main target,

01:14:34.890 --> 01:14:38.630
the main objective, is how
can we improve education.

01:14:38.630 --> 01:14:42.930
Because as I've said in my remarks,

01:14:42.930 --> 01:14:47.923
we have nowadays improved
access to education.

01:14:49.160 --> 01:14:53.400
In the Dominican Republic,
there's 99% access to education.

01:14:53.400 --> 01:14:55.430
The problem is not access anymore.

01:14:55.430 --> 01:14:57.500
The problem is the quality of education.

01:14:57.500 --> 01:15:00.660
Then it has to do with
teacher training programs,

01:15:00.660 --> 01:15:03.460
the use of new technologies,

01:15:03.460 --> 01:15:06.780
the idea of learning a new language,

01:15:06.780 --> 01:15:08.990
of becoming digital literate,

01:15:08.990 --> 01:15:11.090
of having a global outlook,

01:15:11.090 --> 01:15:12.630
because nowadays you have to be,

01:15:12.630 --> 01:15:14.640
you need to have a global outlook.

01:15:14.640 --> 01:15:17.800
It's not only your country,
because you will be interacting

01:15:17.800 --> 01:15:19.660
with different parts of the world.

01:15:19.660 --> 01:15:21.740
Doing it with limited resources,

01:15:21.740 --> 01:15:24.680
as we have had in Latin America,
makes it more difficult.

01:15:24.680 --> 01:15:27.150
But if we have a Latino diaspora,

01:15:27.150 --> 01:15:31.290
that it is interacting with
our respective countries,

01:15:31.290 --> 01:15:32.590
that makes it easier.

01:15:32.590 --> 01:15:35.800
I come to Yale, I talk to
a group of students here,

01:15:35.800 --> 01:15:39.030
and everybody is excited
on the possibility

01:15:39.030 --> 01:15:42.290
of connecting with students
from the Dominican Republic

01:15:42.290 --> 01:15:44.030
in different exchange programs.

01:15:44.030 --> 01:15:46.410
Now, can you imagine a student

01:15:46.410 --> 01:15:49.090
from (speaks foreign language)

01:15:49.090 --> 01:15:53.560
coming for a seminar or
workshop at Yale University?

01:15:53.560 --> 01:15:55.900
This is a major revolution for him.

01:15:55.900 --> 01:15:59.040
He will connect with
professors, new reading lists,

01:15:59.040 --> 01:16:00.750
students that will open up your minds

01:16:00.750 --> 01:16:01.920
with new ways of thinking.

01:16:01.920 --> 01:16:04.700
So when they go back, they
go back with new tools,

01:16:04.700 --> 01:16:06.970
new ways of thinking, new ways of doing.

01:16:06.970 --> 01:16:08.550
They'll revolutionize things.

01:16:08.550 --> 01:16:12.390
This is why I think the best
way to really make an impact

01:16:12.390 --> 01:16:14.040
in terms of economic development

01:16:14.040 --> 01:16:17.020
is through exchange, exchange of ideas.

01:16:17.020 --> 01:16:20.430
You never know exactly
how you touch a person,

01:16:20.430 --> 01:16:22.190
how you change a person's lives,

01:16:22.190 --> 01:16:26.130
just by the sole fact of
exchanging ideas and opinions.

01:16:26.130 --> 01:16:28.830
So I believe in that,
in by having you here,

01:16:28.830 --> 01:16:31.550
the Dominican Students Association,

01:16:31.550 --> 01:16:33.410
and the Latinos Association,

01:16:33.410 --> 01:16:36.120
there's a great wealth of
opportunities for exchange

01:16:36.120 --> 01:16:39.870
and really continuing this
path of social transformation

01:16:39.870 --> 01:16:41.250
in our countries in Latin America

01:16:41.250 --> 01:16:43.313
and the Latino community here in the US.

01:16:45.150 --> 01:16:46.200
- [Jeff] Mr. President, thank you.

01:16:46.200 --> 01:16:47.149
- Thank you.

01:16:47.149 --> 01:16:50.316
(audience applauding)

01:16:58.378 --> 01:16:59.640
- Thank you for coming today.

01:16:59.640 --> 01:17:01.860
I think maybe the
president will be able to

01:17:02.780 --> 01:17:04.470
shake a few hands here for a few minutes,

01:17:04.470 --> 01:17:07.630
but then we have to get
him to another place.

01:17:07.630 --> 01:17:12.210
So good evening, good luck,
enjoy the warm weather.

01:17:12.210 --> 01:17:14.072
He brought it in from Santo Domingo.

01:17:14.072 --> 01:17:15.252
(audience laughing)

01:17:15.252 --> 01:17:18.502
(audience applauding)

